[36m(TaskRunner pid=1880086)[0m ["To convert the point \\((0,3)\\) from rectangular coordinates to polar coordinates, we need to find the values of \\(r\\) and \\(\\theta\\).\n\nThe formulas to convert from rectangular coordinates \\((x, y)\\) to polar coordinates \\((r, \\theta)\\) are:\n\\[ r = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\]\n\\[ \\theta = \\begin{cases} \n\\arctan\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) & \\text{if } x > 0 \\\\\n\\arctan\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) + \\pi & \\text{if } x < 0 \\text{ and } y \\ge 0 \\\\\n\\arctan\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) - \\pi & \\text{if } x < 0 \\text{ and } y < 0 \\\\\n\\frac{\\pi}{2} & \\text{if } x = 0 \\text{ and } y > 0 \\\\\n-\\frac{\\pi}{2} & \\text{if } x = 0 \\text{ and } y < 0 \\\\\n\\text{undefined} & \\text{if } x = 0 \\text{ and } y = 0 \n\\end{cases} \\]\n\nFor the point \\((0,3)\\):\n\\[ r = \\sqrt{0^2 + 3^2} = \\sqrt{9} = 3 \\]\nSince \\(x = 0\\) and \\(y > 0\\), \\(\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\).\n\nSo the polar coordinates are \\((3, \\frac{\\pi}{2})\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Rectangular coordinates\nx = 0\ny = 3\n\n# Calculate r\nr = math.sqrt(x**2 + y**2)\n\n# Calculate theta\nif x == 0 and y > 0:\n    theta = math.pi / 2\nelif x == 0 and y < 0:\n    theta = -math.pi / 2\nelse:\n    theta = math.atan2(y, x)\n\n# Ensure theta is in the range [0, 2*pi)\nif theta < 0:\n    theta += 2 * math.pi\n\nprint(((r, theta)))\n```\n```output\n(3.0, 1.5707963267948966)\n```\nThe polar coordinates of the point \\((0, 3)\\) are \\((3, \\frac{\\pi}{2})\\).\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{(3, \\frac{\\pi}{2})}\n\\]", "To convert the point $(0,3)$ from rectangular coordinates to polar coordinates, we need to find the radius $r$ and the angle $\\theta$.\n\nThe radius $r$ is the distance from the origin to the point, which can be calculated using the formula:\n\\[ r = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\]\nFor the point $(0,3)$, this becomes:\n\\[ r = \\sqrt{0^2 + 3^2} = \\sqrt{9} = 3 \\]\n\nThe angle $\\theta$ is the angle between the positive x-axis and the line segment connecting the origin to the point. For the point $(0,3)$, the angle $\\theta$ is $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ because the point lies directly on the positive y-axis.\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of the point $(0,3)$ are $\\left(3, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$.\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Rectangular coordinates\nx = 0\ny = 3\n\n# Calculate the radius r\nr = math.sqrt(x**2 + y**2)\n\n# Calculate the angle theta\ntheta = math.atan2(y, x)\n\n# Ensure theta is in the range [0, 2*pi)\nif theta < 0:\n    theta += 2 * math.pi\n\nprint(((r, theta)))\n```\n```output\n(3.0, 1.5707963267948966)\n```\nThe polar coordinates of the point $(0,3)$ are $\\left(3, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$. Therefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\left(3, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)}\n\\]", "To convert the point \\((0, 3)\\) from rectangular coordinates to polar coordinates, we need to find the radius \\(r\\) and the angle \\(\\theta\\).\n\nThe formulas to convert from rectangular coordinates \\((x, y)\\) to polar coordinates \\((r, \\theta)\\) are:\n\\[ r = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\]\n\\[ \\theta = \\begin{cases} \n\\arctan\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) & \\text{if } x > 0 \\\\\n\\arctan\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) + \\pi & \\text{if } x < 0 \\text{ and } y \\ge 0 \\\\\n\\arctan\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) - \\pi & \\text{if } x < 0 \\text{ and } y < 0 \\\\\n\\frac{\\pi}{2} & \\text{if } x = 0 \\text{ and } y > 0 \\\\\n-\\frac{\\pi}{2} & \\text{if } x = 0 \\text{ and } y < 0 \\\\\n\\text{undefined} & \\text{if } x = 0 \\text{ and } y = 0 \n\\end{cases} \\]\n\nFor the point \\((0, 3)\\):\n\\[ r = \\sqrt{0^2 + 3^2} = \\sqrt{9} = 3 \\]\nSince \\(x = 0\\) and \\(y > 0\\), the angle \\(\\theta\\) is \\(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\).\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates are \\((3, \\frac{\\pi}{2})\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Rectangular coordinates\nx = 0\ny = 3\n\n# Calculate the radius 