eve this.\n```python\nimport itertools\n\n# Define the expression as a string\nexpression = "2 * 3 * 4 * 5 + 1"\n\n# Function to evaluate the expression with different parentheses placements\ndef evaluate_expression(expr):\n    # Split the expression into different parts\n    parts = expr.split(\' + \')\n    # Evaluate all possible ways to insert parentheses\n    results = set()\n    for i in range(1, len(parts)):\n        left = parts[:i]\n        right = parts[i:]\n        for l in itertools.product(left, repeat=i):\n            for r in itertools.product(right, repeat=len(right)):\n                # Construct the expression with parentheses\n                new_expr = \'(\' + \' * \'.join(l) + \') + (\' + \' * \'.join(r) + \')\'\n                # Evaluate the new expression\n                results.add(eval(new_expr))\n    return results\n\n# Get all unique results\nunique_results = evaluate_expression(expression)\n# Count the number of unique results\nnum_unique_results = len(unique_results)\nprint((num_unique_results, unique_results))\n```\n```output\nTypeError: can only concatenate list (not "str") to list\n```\nIt seems there was an issue with the way we constructed the new expressions with parentheses. We need to ensure that we correctly handle the splitting and concatenation of the expression parts.\n\nLet\'s correct the approach and try again. We will use a more straightforward method to evaluate all possible ways to insert parentheses by considering the different possible placements of parentheses.\n\nHere\'s the revised code:\n```python\nimport itertools\n\n# Define the expression as a string\nexpression = "2 * 3 * 4 * 5 + 1"\n\n# Function to evaluate the expression with different parentheses placements\ndef evaluate_expression(expr):\n    # Split the expression into different parts\n    parts = expr.split(\' + \')\n    # Evaluate all possible ways to insert parentheses\n    results = set()\n    for i in range(1, len(parts)):\n        left = parts[:i]\n