y simplifying the expression step by step in Python.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the complex numbers z and c\nz = 2 + sp.sqrt(2) - (3 + 3*sp.sqrt(2))*sp.I\nc = 2 - 3*sp.I\n\n# Translate z by subtracting c\nz_translated = z - c\n\n# Rotate the translated point by pi/4 counter-clockwise\nrotation_factor = sp.exp(sp.I * sp.pi / 4)\nz_rotated = z_translated * rotation_factor\n\n# Translate the plane back by adding c\nw = z_rotated + c\n\n# Simplify the result\nw_simplified = sp.simplify(w)\nprint(w_simplified)\n```\n```output\n(-1)**(1/4)*(sqrt(2) + (-3 - 3*sqrt(2) - 3*I)*exp(I*pi/4) + 2)\n```\nIt seems like the expression is not simplifying as expected. Let's manually simplify the expression step by step.\n\n1. Translate \\(z\\) by subtracting \\(c\\):\n\\[ z - c = (2 + \\sqrt{2} - (3 + 3\\sqrt{2})i) - (2 - 3i) = \\sqrt{2} - 3\\sqrt{2}i \\]\n\n2. Rotate the translated point by \\(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\) counter-clockwise:\n\\[ (\\sqrt{2} - 3\\sqrt{2}i) \\cdot \\frac{1+i}{\\sqrt{2}} = (\\sqrt{2} - 3\\sqrt{2}i) \\cdot \\frac{1+i}{\\sqrt{2}} = (1 - 3i)(1 + i) = 1 + i - 3i + 3 = 4 - 2i \\]\n\n3. Translate the plane back by adding \\(c\\):\n\\[ w = 4 - 2i + (2 - 3i) = 6 - 5i \\]\n\nLet's verify this by manually simplifying the expression in Python using sympy.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the complex numbers z and c\nz = 2 + sp.sqrt(2) - (3 + 3*sp.sqrt(2))*sp.I\nc = 2 - 3*sp.I\n\n# Translate z by subtracting c\nz_translated = z - c\n\n# Rotate the translated point by pi/4 counter-clockwise\nrotation_factor = sp.exp(sp.I * sp.pi / 4)\nz_rotated = z_translated * rotation_factor\n\n# Translate the plane back by adding c\nw = z_rotated + c\n\n# Simplify the result\nw_simplified = sp.simplify(w)\nprint(w_simplified)\n```\n```output\n(-1)**(1/4)*(sqrt(2) + (-3 - 3*sqrt(2) - 3*I)*exp(I*pi/4) + 2)\n```\n\nReach max function call limit.", "To solve the problem of rotating a complex number $z$ around another complex number $c$ by an angle $\\theta$, we can use the formula for complex number rotation:\n\n\\[ w = c + (z - c) \\cdot e^{i\\theta} \\]\n\nIn this case, $z = 2 + \\sqrt{2} - (3 + 3\\sqrt{2})i$, $c = 2 - 3i$, and $\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{4}$. The rotation factor $e^{i\\theta}$ for $\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{4}$ is $e^{i\\frac{\\pi}{4}} = \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) + i\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + i\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$.\n\nFirst, let's compute $z - c$:\n\n\\[ z - c = (2 + \\sqrt{2} - (3 + 3\\sqrt{2})i) - (2 - 3i) = \\sqrt{2} + (-3 - 3\\sqrt{2} + 3)i = \\sqrt{2} - 3\\sqrt{2}i \\]\n\nNext, we multiply $z - c$ by $e^{i\\frac{\\pi}{4}}$:\n\n\\[ (z - c) \\cdot e^{i\\frac{\\pi}{4}} = (\\sqrt{2} - 3\\sqrt{2}i) \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + i\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\]\n\nLet's compute this product step-by-step:\n\n\\[ (\\sqrt{2} - 3\\sqrt{2}i) \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + i\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) = \\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + \\sqrt{2} \\cdot i\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} - 3\\sqrt{2}i \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} - 3\\sqrt{2}i \\cdot i\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\]\n\\[ = 1 + i - 3i + 3 = 4 - 2i \\]\n\nFinally, we add $c$ to the result:\n\n\\[ w = c + (z - c) \\cdot e^{i\\frac{\\pi}{4}} = (2 - 3i) + (4 - 2i) = 6 - 5i \\]\n\nSo the final answer is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{6 - 5i} \\]\n\nLet's verify this using Python code.\n```python\nimport cmath\r\n\r\n# Define the complex numbers z and c\r\nz = 2 + cmath.sqrt(2) - (3 + 3 * cmath.sqrt(2)) * 1j\r\nc = 2 - 3 * 1j\r\n\r\n# Define the rotation angle\r\ntheta = cmath.pi / 4\r\n\r\n# Compute the rotation factor\r\nrotation_factor = cmath.exp(1j * theta)\r\n\r\n# Compute the rotated complex number w\r\nw = c + (z - c) * rotation_factor\r\nprint(w)\n```", "The given expression is a series of alternating positive and negative integers starting from 1 to 100. We can group the terms in pairs as follows:\n\n\\[\n(1 - 2) + (3 - 4) + (5 - 6) + \\dots + (99 - 100)\n\\]\n\nEach pair $(1 - 2)$, $(3 - 4)$, $(5 - 6)$, and so on, evaluates to $-1$. 