 \\).\n\nThe decrease in area is \\( s^2 - 0.90s^2 = 0.10s^2 \\). To find the percent decrease in area, we divide the decrease in area by the original area and multiply by 100:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Percent decrease} = \\left( \\frac{0.10s^2}{s^2} \\right) \\times 100 = 10\\%\n\\]\n\nLet's verify this calculation using Python code.\n```python\n# Let's define the original side length of the square as s\ns = 1  # The actual value of s doesn't matter because it will cancel out\n\n# Calculate the new dimensions of the rectangle\nnew_length = s * (1 - 0.40)\nnew_width = s * (1 + 0.50)\n\n# Calculate the original and new areas\noriginal_area = s ** 2\nnew_area = new_length * new_width\n\n# Calculate the percent decrease in area\npercent_decrease = ((original_area - new_area) / original_area) * 100\nprint(percent_decrease)\n```\n```output\n10.0\n```\nThe percent decrease in the area of the square is \\(\\boxed{10}\\)%.", "Let's start by defining the side length of the original square as \\( s \\). The area of the original square is \\( s^2 \\).\n\nWhen two opposite sides of the square are decreased by 40%, their new length becomes \\( s \\times (1 - 0.40) = 0.60s \\). When the other two sides are increased by 50%, their new length becomes \\( s \\times (1 + 0.50) = 1.50s \\).\n\nThe area of the resulting rectangle is the product of these new side lengths: \\( 0.60s \\times 1.50s = 0.90s^2 \\).\n\nThe decrease in area is the original area minus the new area: \\( s^2 - 0.90s^2 = 0.10s^2 \\).\n\nThe percent decrease in area is the decrease in area divided by the original area, multiplied by 100: \\( \\frac{0.10s^2}{s^2} \\times 100 = 10\\% \\).\n\nSo, the square's area decreases by \\( 10\\% \\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Define the side length of the original square\ns = 1  # The value of s does not matter as it will cancel out\n\n# Calculate the new side lengths\nnew_side1 = s * (1 - 0.40)\nnew_side2 = s * (1 + 0.50)\n\n# Calculate the original and new 