satisfies all the equations. Therefore, the ordered pair \\((a, b)\\) is \\((-2, 1)\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code to ensure the result is accurate.\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, Eq, solve\n\n# Define the variables\na, b, x = symbols('a b x')\n\n# Define the function f(x)\nf_x = x**3 + 3*x**2 + 1\n\n# Define the left-hand side f(x) - f(a)\nlhs = f_x - (a**3 + 3*a**2 + 1)\n\n# Define the right-hand side (x - a)^2 * (x - b)\nrhs = (x - a)**2 * (x - b)\n\n# Expand the right-hand side\nrhs_expanded = rhs.expand()\n\n# Compare coefficients of the polynomials\ncoeffs_lhs = lhs.as_coefficients_dict()\ncoeffs_rhs = rhs_expanded.as_coefficients_dict()\n\n# Create equations by comparing coefficients\neq1 = Eq(coeffs_rhs[x**2], coeffs_lhs[x**2])  # Coefficient of x^2\neq2 = Eq(coeffs_rhs[x], coeffs_lhs[x])       # Coefficient of x\neq3 = Eq(coeffs_rhs[1], coeffs_lhs[1])       # Constant term\n\n# Solve the system of equations\nsolution = solve((eq1, eq2, eq3), (a, b))\nprint(solution)\n```\n```output\n[]\n```\nIt seems that the direct symbolic comparison did not yield any solutions, possibly due to the complexity of the expressions. Let's take a more manual approach to verify our solution step-by-step using Python.\n\nWe'll substitute \\(a = -2\\) and \\(b = 1\\) into the original equation and check if both sides are equal.\n\n1. Compute \\(f(x) - f(a)\\) with \\(a = -2\\).\n2. Compute \\((x - a)^2 (x - b)\\) with \\(a = -2\\) and \\(b = 1\\).\n3. Check if both sides are equal.\n\nLet's do this step-by-step in Python.\n```python\n# Define the function f(x)\ndef f(x):\n    return x**3 + 3*x**2 + 1\n\n# Define the values of a and b\na = -2\nb = 1\n\n# Compute f(x) - f(a)\nlhs = lambda x: f(x) - f(a)\n\n# Compute (x - a)^2 * (x - b)\nrhs = lambda x: (x - a)**2 * (x - b)\n\n# Check if both sides are equal for a range of x values\nx_values = range(-10, 11)  # Test for a range of x values\nresults = [(x, lhs(x) == rhs(x)) for x in x_values]\nprint(results)\n```\