  # Update the maximum slope\n        if slope > max_slope:\n            max_slope = slope\n\nprint(max_slope)\n```\n```output\n1.5\n```\nThe output from the code indicates that the greatest possible value of the slope of the line containing points \\( A \\) and \\( B \\) is \\( 1.5 \\). This can be expressed as the common fraction \\( \\frac{3}{2} \\).\n\nTo confirm, let's reason through the problem again:\n\nThe coordinates of point \\( A \\) range from \\((0,0)\\) to \\((2,2)\\), and the coordinates of point \\( B \\) range from \\((4,2)\\) to \\((5,3)\\). The maximum slope will occur when the change in \\( y \\) is maximized relative to the change in \\( x \\). The point \\((0,0)\\) in square \\( A \\) and the point \\((5,3)\\) in square \\( B \\) will give the maximum slope:\n\n\\[ m = \\frac{3 - 0}{5 - 0} = \\frac{3}{5} \\]\n\nHowever, another combination of points can give a greater slope. The point \\((0,2)\\) in square \\( A \\) and the point \\((4,3)\\) in square \\( B \\) will give:\n\n\\[ m = \\frac{3 - 2}{4 - 0} = \\frac{1}{4} \\]\n\nThe point \\((2,0)\\) in square \\( A \\) and the point \\((5,3)\\) in square \\( B \\) will give:\n\n\\[ m = \\frac{3 - 0}{5 - 2} = \\frac{3}{3} = 1 \\]\n\nThe point \\((2,2)\\) in square \\( A \\) and the point \\((4,3)\\) in square \\( B \\) will give:\n\n\\[ m = \\frac{3 - 2}{4 - 2} = \\frac{1}{2} \\]\n\nThe point \\((2,2)\\) in square \\( A \\) and the point \\((5,3)\\) in square \\( B \\) will give:\n\n\\[ m = \\frac{3 - 2}{5 - 2} = \\frac{1}{3} \\]\n\nThe point \\((0,0)\\) in square \\( A \\) and the point \\((4,3)\\) in square \\( B \\) will give:\n\n\\[ m = \\frac{3 - 0}{4 - 0} = \\frac{3}{4} \\]\n\nThe point \\((0,0)\\) in square \\( A \\) and the point \\((5,2)\\) in square \\( B \\) will give:\n\n\\[ m = \\frac{2 - 0}{5 - 0} = \\frac{2}{5} \\]\n\nThe point \\((0,0)\\) in square \\( A \\) and the point \\((5,3)\\) in square \\( B \\) will give:\n\n\\[ m = \\frac{3 - 0}{5 - 0} = \\frac{3}{5} \\]\n\nThe point \\((0