xt{GCD}(378, 255) \\).\n3. \\(378 \\div 255 = 1\\) with remainder 123, so \\( \\text{GCD}(378, 255) = \\text{GCD}(255, 123) \\).\n4. \\(255 \\div 123 = 2\\) with remainder 9, so \\( \\text{GCD}(255, 123) = \\text{GCD}(123, 9) \\).\n5. \\(123 \\div 9 = 13\\) with remainder 6, so \\( \\text{GCD}(123, 9) = \\text{GCD}(9, 6) \\).\n6. \\(9 \\div 6 = 1\\) with remainder 3, so \\( \\text{GCD}(9, 6) = \\text{GCD}(6, 3) \\).\n7. \\(6 \\div 3 = 2\\) with remainder 0, so \\( \\text{GCD}(6, 3) = 3 \\).\n\nThus, the GCD of 3339 and 2961 is 213. Now we need to find the GCD of 213 and 1491.\n\n1. Divide 1491 by 213 to get the quotient 7 and remainder 0. So, \\( \\text{GCD}(1491, 213) = 213 \\).\n\nThus, the GCD of 3339, 2961, and 1491 is indeed 213. The correct answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{213}\n\\]", "To find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of the numbers 3339, 2961, and 1491, we can use the Euclidean algorithm. The Euclidean algorithm is an efficient method for computing the greatest common divisor of two numbers. We can extend this method to more than two numbers by iteratively applying it.\n\nHere's the step-by-step approach:\n\n1. First, we find the GCD of 3339 and 2961.\n2. Then, we find the GCD of the result from step 1 and 1491.\n\nWe'll use Python to perform these calculations. Let's start by implementing the Euclidean algorithm to find the GCD of two numbers, and then extend it to find the GCD of three numbers.\n```python\ndef gcd(a, b):\r\n    while b:\r\n        a, b = b, a % b\r\n    return a\r\n\r\n# Given numbers\r\nnum1 = 3339\r\nnum2 = 2961\r\nnum3 = 1491\r\n\r\n# Find GCD of num1 and num2\r\ngcd_1_2 = gcd(num1, num2)\r\n\r\n# Find GCD of gcd_1_2 and num3\r\ngcd_final = gcd(gcd_1_2, num3)\r\n\r\nprint(gcd_final)\n```\n```output\n21\n```\nThe greatest common divisor (GCD) of 3339, 2961, and 1491 is \\(\\boxed{21}\\).", "To find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 3339, 2961, and 1491, we can use the Euclidean algorithm. The Euclidean algorithm is an efficient method for computing the greatest common divisor of two numbers. To find the GCD of more than two numbers, we can iteratively apply the Euclidean algorithm.\n\nHere's the step-by-step process:\n\n1. Find the GCD of the first two numbers, 3339 and 2961.\n2. Find the GCD of the result from step 1 and the third number, 1491.\n\nLet's implement this in Python using the `math.gcd` function from the `math` module.\n\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Define the numbers\na = 3339\nb = 2961\nc = 1491\n\n# Find the GCD of the first two numbers\ngcd_ab = math.gcd(a, b)\n\n# Find the GCD of the result and the third number\ngcd_abc = math.gcd(gcd_ab, c)\n\n# Print the final result\nprint(gcd_abc)\n```\n```output\n21\n```\nThe greatest common divisor (GCD) of 3339, 2961, and 1491 is \\(\\boxed{21}\\).", "To find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of three numbers, we can use the Euclidean algorithm. The Euclidean algorithm is an efficient method for computing the greatest common divisor (GCD) of two numbers, the largest number that divides both of them without leaving a remainder. The algorithm is based on the principle that the GCD of two numbers also divides their difference.\n\nFirst, we will find the GCD of the first two numbers, 3339 and 2961. Then, we will find the GCD of the result and the third number, 1491. Let's start by implementing the Euclidean algorithm in Python and finding the GCD of 3339 and 2961. Then we will find the GCD of the result with 1491.\n\nHere is the step-by-step process:\n\n1. Find the GCD of 3339 and 2961.\n2. Use the result from step 1 to find the GCD with 1491.\n\nLet's write the Python code to do this.\n```python\ndef gcd(a, b):\r\n    while b:\r\n        a, b = b, a % b\r\n    return a\r\n\r\n# Step 1: Find the GCD of 3339 and 2961\r\ngcd_3339_2961 = gcd(3339, 2961)\r\n\r\n# Step 2: Find the GCD of the result from step 1 with 1491\r\ngcd_result = gcd(gcd_3339_2961, 1491)\r\n\r\nprint(gcd_result)\n```\n```output\n21\n```\nThe greatest common divisor (GCD) 