he expected projection\r\nprint((projection, expected_projection))\n```\n```output\n([0.8333333333333334, -1.6666666666666667, 0.8333333333333334], [0.8333333333333334, -1.6666666666666667, 0.8333333333333334])\n```\nThe calculated projection \\([0.8333, -1.6667, 0.8333]\\) matches the expected projection \\([0.8333, -1.6667, 0.8333]\\), confirming that our solution is correct. Therefore, the value of \\( y \\) is indeed \\(\\boxed{-4}\\).", "To solve for \\( y \\) in the given problem, we need to use the formula for the projection of a vector \\(\\mathbf{v}\\) onto a vector \\(\\mathbf{u}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{u}} \\mathbf{v} = \\frac{\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}}{\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{u}} \\mathbf{u}\n\\]\n\nGiven:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ y \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{u} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\nThe projection is:\n\\[\n\\frac{5}{6} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we calculate the dot product \\(\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = 1 \\cdot 2 + (-2) \\cdot y + 1 \\cdot (-5) = 2 - 2y - 5 = -3 - 2y\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate the dot product \\(\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{u}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = 1^2 + (-2)^2 + 1^2 = 1 + 4 + 1 = 6\n\\]\n\nUsing the projection formula, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{-3 - 2y}{6} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\frac{5}{6} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThis implies:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{-3 - 2y}{6} = \\frac{5}{6}\n\\]\n\nWe can solve for \\( y \\) by equating the scalar multipliers:\n\n\\[\n-3 - 2y = 5\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\( y \\):\n\n\\[\n-2y = 5 + 3\n\\]\n\\[\n-2y = 8\n\\]\n\\[\ny = -4\n\\]\n\nLet's verify this using Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, Eq, solve\r\n\r\n# Define the variable\r\ny = symbols('y')\r\n\r\n# Define the equation based on the projection formula\r\nequation = Eq((-3