titute these \\(y\\)-values back into one of the original equations to find the corresponding \\(x\\)-values. We'll use the first equation \\(x^2 + y^2 + 6x - 24y + 72 = 0\\):\n\nFor \\(y = 10 + \\sqrt{41}\\):\n\n\\[\nx^2 + (10 + \\sqrt{41})^2 + 6x - 24(10 + \\sqrt{41}) + 72 = 0\n\\]\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n\\[\nx^2 + 100 + 20\\sqrt{41} + 41 + 6x - 240 - 24\\sqrt{41} + 72 = 0\n\\]\n\nCombining like terms, we get:\n\n\\[\nx^2 + 6x - 27 - 4\\sqrt{41} = 0\n\\]\n\nThis is a quadratic equation in \\(x\\). We can solve it using the quadratic formula \\(x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\), where \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = 6\\), and \\(c = -27 - 4\\sqrt{41}\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-6 \\pm \\sqrt{36 + 4(27 + 4\\sqrt{41})}}{2} = \\frac{-6 \\pm \\sqrt{36 + 108 + 16\\sqrt{41}}}{2} = \\frac{-6 \\pm \\sqrt{144 + 16\\sqrt{41}}}{2} = \\frac{-6 \\pm 4\\sqrt{9 + \\sqrt{41}}}{2} = -3 \\pm 2\\sqrt{9 + \\sqrt{41}}\n\\]\n\nSo, the \\(x\\)-coordinates of the intersection points are \\(x = -3 + 2\\sqrt{9 + \\sqrt{41}}\\) and \\(x = -3 - 2\\sqrt{9 + \\sqrt{41}}\\).\n\nSimilarly, for \\(y = 10 - \\sqrt{41}\\):\n\n\\[\nx^2 + (10 - \\sqrt{41})^2 + 6x - 24(10 - \\sqrt{41}) + 72 = 0\n\\]\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n\\[\nx^2 + 100 - 20\\sqrt{41} + 41 + 6x - 240 + 24\\sqrt{41} + 72 = 0\n\\]\n\nCombining like terms, we get:\n\n\\[\nx^2 + 6x - 27 + 4\\sqrt{41} = 0\n\\]\n\nThis is a quadratic equation in \\(x\\). We can solve it using the quadratic formula \\(x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\), where \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = 6\\), and \\(c = -27 + 4\\sqrt{41}\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-6 \\pm \\sqrt{36 + 4(27 - 4\\sqrt{41})}}{2} = \\frac{-6 \\pm \\sqrt{36 + 108 - 16\\sqrt{41}}}{2} = \\frac{-6 \\pm \\sqrt{144 - 16\\sqrt{41}}}{2} = \\frac{-6 \\pm 4\\sqrt{9 - \\sqrt{41}}}{2} = -3 \\pm 2\\sqrt{9 - \\sqrt{41}}\n\\]\n\nSo, the \\(x\\)-coordinates of the intersection points are \\(x = -3 + 2\\sqrt{9 - \\sqrt{41}}\\) and \\(x = -3 - 2\\sqrt{9 - \\sqrt{41}}\\).\n\nThus, the four intersection points are:\