 of parentheses.\n2. Iterate through different possible placements of parentheses.\n3. Evaluate each placement and keep track of the minimum value.\n\nLet\'s implement this in Python.\n```python\nimport itertools\n\n# Define the expression as a string\nexpression = "1 + 2 + 3 - 4 + 5 + 6"\n\n# Define possible placements of parentheses\n# We will use \'()\' to denote the placement of parentheses\n# For example, "(1 + 2) + 3 - 4 + 5 + 6" is one possible placement\n# We will generate all possible placements by inserting parentheses in different positions\n\n# Function to evaluate the expression for a given placement of parentheses\ndef evaluate_expression(expr):\n    try:\n        return eval(expr)\n    except:\n        return float(\'inf\')  # Return a large value if there\'s an error (e.g., division by zero)\n\n# Generate all possible placements of parentheses\n# We will use a list of all possible positions to insert parentheses\npositions = list(range(1, len(expression) - 1))\nmin_value = float(\'inf\')\nmin_expr = ""\n\n# Iterate through all possible combinations of positions to insert parentheses\nfor r in range(1, len(positions) + 1):\n    for combo in itertools.combinations(positions, r):\n        # Create a copy of the expression\n        expr = expression[:]\n        # Insert parentheses at the specified positions\n        for pos in combo:\n            expr = expr[:pos] + "(" + expr[pos:] + ")"\n        # Evaluate the expression\n        value = evaluate_expression(expr)\n        # Update the minimum value if the current value is smaller\n        if value < min_value:\n            min_value = value\n            min_expr = expr\n\nprint(min_value)\n```\n```output\n-1\n```\nThe minimum possible value of the expression \\(1 + 2 + 3 - 4 + 5 + 6\\) when we only add parentheses (and not rearrange the order of the terms) is \\(\\boxed{-1}\\).', "To solve this problem, we need to explore different ways of placing parentheses in the expression \\(1 + 2 + 3 - 4 + 5 + 6