\\frac{37264}{37265}i\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{37413}{37265} - \\frac{37264}{37265}i\\right)\\left(\\frac{37413}{37265} + \\frac{37264}{37265}i\\right)} = \\frac{\\frac{1399500569}{1388695225} + \\frac{1399500568}{1388695225}i + \\frac{1399500568}{1388695225}i - \\frac{1399500568}{1388695225}}{\\frac{1399500569}{1388695225} + \\frac{1399500568}{1388695225}} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{1388695225} + \\frac{2799001136}{1388695225}i}{\\frac{2799001137}{1388695225}} = \\frac{1}{2799001137} + \\frac{2799001136}{2799001137}i\n   \\]\n\nIt seems that the terms are becoming more complex, but we can notice a pattern. Let\'s simplify the process by checking if there is a cycle in the sequence. We can use a numerical approach to check the first few terms.\n\nLet\'s use Python to compute the first few terms numerically and check for a cycle.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the function F(z)\ndef F(z):\n    return (z + sp.I) / (z - sp.I)\n\n# Initial value z0\nz0 = sp.Rational(1, 137) + sp.I\n\n# Compute the first few terms of the sequence\nz1 = F(z0)\nz2 = F(z1)\nz3 = F(z2)\nz4 = F(z3)\nz5 = F(z4)\nz6 = F(z5)\n\n# Convert to numerical values and print\nz0_num = z0.evalf()\nz1_num = z1.evalf()\nz2_num = z2.evalf()\nz3_num = z3.evalf()\nz4_num = z4.evalf()\nz5_num = z5.evalf()\nz6_num = z6.evalf()\n\nprint(f"z0 = {z0_num}")\nprint(f"z1 = {z1_num}")\nprint(f"z2 = {z2_num}")\nprint(f"z3 = {z3_num}")\nprint(f"z4 = {z4_num}")\nprint(f"z5 = {z5_num}")\nprint(f"z6 = {z6_num}")\n```\n```output\nz0 = 0.00729927007299270 + 1.0*I\nz1 = 1.00000000000000 + 274.0*I\nz2 = 1.01301301301301 + 0.00371223881253250*I\nz3 = 0.99999999999...15789473684 + 1.00000000000000*I\nz5 = 0.00729927007299270 + 1.00000000000000*I\nz6 = 1.00000000000000 + 274.0*I\n```\nFrom the numerical output, we can observe that the sequence appears to be cycling every 4 terms. Specifically, we have:\n\n\\[\nz_0 = 0.00729927007299270 + 1.0i\n\\]\n\\[\nz_1 = 1.0 + 274.0i\n\\]\n\\[\nz_2 = 1.01301301301301 + 0.0037122388125325