est angle of the quadrilateral as \\( x \\) degrees. Since the largest angle is twice the smallest angle, it will be \\( 2x \\) degrees. The other two angles are both right angles, so they are each \\( 90 \\) degrees.\n\nThe sum of the interior angles of a quadrilateral is always \\( 360 \\) degrees. So, we can set up the following equation to represent the sum of the angles in this quadrilateral:\n\n\\[ x + 2x + 90 + 90 = 360 \\]\n\nSimplifying the left side, we get:\n\n\\[ 3x + 180 = 360 \\]\n\nSubtracting 180 from both sides, we get:\n\n\\[ 3x = 180 \\]\n\nDividing both sides by 3, we get:\n\n\\[ x = 60 \\]\n\nSo, the smallest angle is \\( 60 \\) degrees. The largest angle, which is twice the smallest angle, is \\( 2 \\times 60 = 120 \\) degrees.\n\nThus, the measure of the largest angle is \\( \\boxed{120} \\) degrees.", "Let's denote the smallest angle by \\(x\\). Since the largest angle is twice the smallest angle, the largest angle will be \\(2x\\). The other two angles are both right angles, so they each measure \\(90^\\circ\\).\n\nThe sum of the interior angles of a quadrilateral is always \\(360^\\circ\\). Therefore, we can set up the following equation:\n\n\\[x + 2x + 90 + 90 = 360\\]\n\nSimplifying the left side, we get:\n\n\\[3x + 180 = 360\\]\n\nSubtract 180 from both sides of the equation:\n\n\\[3x = 180\\]\n\nDivide both sides by 3:\n\n\\[x = 60\\]\n\nSo the smallest angle is \\(60^\\circ\\). The largest angle, which is twice the smallest angle, is \\(2 \\times 60 = 120^\\circ\\).\n\nThus, the measure of the largest angle is \\(\\boxed{120}\\).\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Define the smallest angle as x\nx = 60\n\n# Calculate the largest angle\nlargest_angle = 2 * x\n\n# Verify the sum of the angles\nangles_sum = x + largest_angle + 90 + 90\n\n# Output the largest angle and the sum of the angles\nprint((largest_angle, angles_sum))\n```", "Let's break down the problem step by step. We know that the sum of the