 is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{11}\n\\]", "To solve the equation \\(\\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{4}{x-5}\\), we can cross-multiply to eliminate the fractions. This gives us:\n\n\\[2(x - 5) = 4 \\cdot 3.\\]\n\nSimplifying the right-hand side, we get:\n\n\\[2(x - 5) = 12.\\]\n\nNext, we divide both sides by 2:\n\n\\[x - 5 = 6.\\]\n\nFinally, we add 5 to both sides to solve for \\(x\\):\n\n\\[x = 11.\\]\n\nLet's verify this solution by substituting \\(x = 11\\) back into the original equation to ensure it holds true. We'll use Python to confirm our result.\n```python\n# Define the value of x\nx = 11\n\n# Calculate the left-hand side and right-hand side of the equation\nlhs = 2 / 3\nrhs = 4 / (x - 5)\n\n# Check if the left-hand side equals the right-hand side\nprint((lhs, rhs, lhs == rhs))\n```\n```output\n(0.6666666666666666, 0.6666666666666666, True)\n```\nThe left-hand side of the equation \\(\\frac{2}{3}\\) is equal to the right-hand side \\(\\frac{4}{x-5}\\) when \\(x = 11\\), confirming that our solution is correct.\n\nThus, the solution to the equation is \\(\\boxed{11}\\).", "To solve the equation $\\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{4}{x-5}$ for $x$, we can use the method of cross-multiplication. This method involves multiplying the numerator of the first fraction by the denominator of the second fraction and setting it equal to the product of the numerator of the second fraction and the denominator of the first fraction. Here's the step-by-step solution:\n\n1. Start with the equation:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{4}{x-5}\n   \\]\n\n2. Cross-multiply to get:\n   \\[\n   2(x-5) = 4 \\cdot 3\n   \\]\n\n3. Simplify the right-hand side:\n   \\[\n   2(x-5) = 12\n   \\]\n\n4. Divide both sides by 2:\n   \\[\n   x-5 = 6\n   \\]\n\n5. Add 5 to both sides to solve for $x$:\n   \\[\n   x = 11\n   \\]\n\nLet's verify this solution using Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, Eq, solve\n\n# Define the variable\nx = symbols('x')\n\n# Define the equation\nequation = Eq