simplified = term2.simplify()\r\n\r\n# Combine the terms into a single fraction\r\ncombined_fraction = term1_simplified + term2_simplified\r\ncombined_fraction_simplified = combined_fraction.simplify()\r\n\r\n# Extract the numerator and denominator\r\nnumerator = combined_fraction_simplified.as_numer_denom()[0]\r\ndenominator = combined_fraction_simplified.as_numer_denom()[1]\r\n\r\n# Extract the coefficients a, b, and c\r\na = numerator.coeff(sqrt(2))\r\nb = numerator.coeff(sqrt(3))\r\nc = denominator\r\n\r\n# Calculate a + b + c\r\nresult = a + b + c\r\nprint((a, b, c, result))\n```\n```output\nTypeError: invalid input: sqrt(2)\n```\nIt seems there was an issue with the way the terms were defined and simplified. Let\'s correct the approach by explicitly handling the square roots and fractions. We\'ll use the `sympy` library to handle the algebraic simplification and combination of the terms.\n\nHere\'s the corrected approach:\n\n1. Define the terms $\\sqrt{2} + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}$ and $\\sqrt{3} + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$.\n2. Simplify each term.\n3. Combine the simplified terms into a single fraction.\n4. Ensure the fraction is in its simplest form.\n5. Extract the coefficients $a$, $b$, and $c$.\n6. Compute $a + b + c$.\n\nLet\'s implement this step-by-step in Python.\n```python\nfrom sympy import sqrt, Rational, simplify\r\n\r\n# Define the terms\r\nterm1 = sqrt(2) + Rational(1, sqrt(2))\r\nterm2 = sqrt(3) + Rational(1, sqrt(3))\r\n\r\n# Simplify the terms\r\nterm1_simplified = simplify(term1)\r\nterm2_simplified = simplify(term2)\r\n\r\n# Combine the terms into a single fraction\r\ncombined_fraction = term1_simplified + term2_simplified\r\ncombined_fraction_simplified = simplify(combined_fraction)\r\n\r\n# Extract the numerator and denominator\r\nnumerator = combined_fraction_simplified.as_numer_denom()[0]\r\ndenominator = combined_fraction_simplified.as_numer_denom()[1]\r\n\r\n# Extract the coefficients a, b, and c\r\na = numerator.coeff(sqrt(2))\r\nb = numera