d \\(q\\) is a factor of the leading coefficient \\(a_n\\).\n\nFor the polynomial \\(2x^4 + a_3 x^3 + a_2 x^2 + a_1 x + 1 = 0\\):\n\n- The constant term is 1, so the possible values for \\(p\\) are \\(\\pm 1\\).\n- The leading coefficient is 2, so the possible values for \\(q\\) are \\(\\pm 1, \\pm 2\\).\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\pm 1}{\\pm 1}, \\frac{\\pm 1}{\\pm 2} \\implies \\pm 1, \\pm \\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\nSo the possible rational roots are \\(1, -1, \\frac{1}{2}, -\\frac{1}{2}\\). There are 4 different possible rational roots.\n\nLet's confirm this using Python code.\n```python\n# Possible values for p (factors of the constant term 1)\np_values = [1, -1]\n\n# Possible values for q (factors of the leading coefficient 2)\nq_values = [1, -1, 2, -2]\n\n# Generate all possible rational roots\npossible_rational_roots = set()\nfor p in p_values:\n    for q in q_values:\n        possible_rational_roots.add(p / q)\n\n# Number of different possible rational roots\nnum_possible_rational_roots = len(possible_rational_roots)\nprint((possible_rational_roots, num_possible_rational_roots))\n```\n```output\n({0.5, 1.0, -0.5, -1.0}, 4)\n```\nThe possible rational roots of the polynomial \\(2x^4 + a_3 x^3 + a_2 x^2 + a_1 x + 1 = 0\\) are \\(1, -1, \\frac{1}{2}, -\\frac{1}{2}\\). There are 4 different possible rational roots.\n\nThus, the number of different possible rational roots of this polynomial is \\(\\boxed{4}\\).", "The Rational Root Theorem states that if a polynomial has integer coefficients, then every rational root of the polynomial can be expressed in the form $\\frac{p}{q}$, where $p$ is a factor of the constant term and $q$ is a factor of the leading coefficient.\n\nFor the polynomial $2x^4 + a_3 x^3 + a_2 x^2 + a_1 x + 1 = 0$, the constant term is 1 and the leading coefficient is 2. The factors of the constant term (1) are $\\pm 1$, and the factors of the leading coefficient (2) are $\\pm 1, \\pm 2$. Therefore, the possible ratio