$, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\sin 20^\\circ \\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 60^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ = \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8}\\right) \\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) = \\frac{3}{16}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the original product is:\n\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{3}{16}\\right)^2 = \\frac{9}{256}.\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{9}{256}}\n\\]\n\nLet's verify this using Python code to ensure the accuracy of the result.\n```python\nimport math\r\n\r\n# Calculate the product of the sines\r\nproduct = (math.sin(math.radians(20)) * \r\n           math.sin(math.radians(40)) * \r\n           math.sin(math.radians(60)) * \r\n           math.sin(math.radians(80)) * \r\n           math.sin(math.radians(100)) * \r\n           math.sin(math.radians(120)) * \r\n           math.sin(math.radians(140)) * \r\n           math.sin(math.radians(160)))\r\n\r\n# Display the result\r\nprint(product)\n```", "To solve the problem, we need to find the product \\(\\sin 20^\\circ \\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 60^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ \\sin 100^\\circ \\sin 120^\\circ \\sin 140^\\circ \\sin 160^\\circ\\).\n\nWe can use the following trigonometric identities and properties:\n1. \\(\\sin(180^\\circ - x) = \\sin x\\).\n2. \\(\\sin 60^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\).\n3. The product of sines of angles in the form \\(\\sin 20^\\circ \\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ \\sin 160^\\circ\\) can be simplified using the identity \\(\\sin x \\sin (60^\\circ - x) \\sin (60^\\circ + x) = \\frac{1}{4} \\sin 3x\\).\n\nLet's break down the problem step-by-step:\n\n1. Use the identity \\(\\sin(180^\\circ - x) = \\sin x\\) to rewrite the angles:\n   \\[\n   \\sin 100^\\circ = \\sin (180^\\circ - 80^\\circ) = \\sin 80^\\circ,\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\sin 120^\\circ = \\sin (180^\\circ - 60^\\circ) = \\sin 60^\\circ,\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\sin 140^\\circ = \\sin (180^\\circ - 40^\\circ) = \\sin 40^\\circ,\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\sin 160^\\circ = \\sin (180^\\circ - 20^\\circ) = \\sin 20^\\circ.\n   \\]\n\n2. Substitute these into the original product:\n   \\[\n   \\sin 20^\\circ \\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 60^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ \\sin 100^\\circ \\sin 120^\\circ \\sin 140^\\circ \\sin 160^\\circ = (\\sin 20^\\circ \\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ \\sin 160^\\circ) (\\sin 60^\\circ)^2 (\\sin 100^\\circ \\sin 120^\\circ \\sin 140^\\circ \\sin 160^\\circ).\n   \\]\n\n3. Simplify using the identity \\(\\sin x \\sin (60^\\circ - x) \\sin (60^\\circ + x) = \\frac{1}{4} \\sin 3x\\):\n   \\[\n   \\sin 20^\\circ \\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ \\sin 160^\\circ = \\frac{1}{4} \\sin 60^\\circ \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\sin 120^\\circ = \\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)^2 \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) = \\frac{3}{64}.\n   \\]\n\n4. Substitute \\(\\sin 60^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\) and simplify:\n   \\[\n   (\\sin 60^\\circ)^2 = \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^2 = \\frac{3}{4}.\n   \\]\n\n5. Combine all parts:\n   \\[\n   \\left(\\frac{3}{64}\\right) \\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right) = \\frac{9}{256}.\n   \\]\n\nLet's implement this in Python to verify the result.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the angles in degrees\nangles = [20, 40, 60, 80, 100, 120, 140, 160]\n\n# Convert degrees to radians and calculate the product of sines\nproduct = 1\nfor angle in angles:\n    product *= sp.sin(sp.rad(angle))\n\n# Simplify the product\nsimplified_product = sp.simplify(product)\nprint(simplified_product)\n```\n```output\n-cos(pi/9)/8 + 1/16 + sin(7*pi/18)/8\n```\nIt appears that the symbolic computation returned an expression that is not fully simplified to a numerical value. Let's take a different approach to simplify the expression manually and verify the result.\n\nWe can use the known trigonometric identities and properties to simplify the product step-by-step. Here's the detailed reasoning:\n\n1. Use the identity \\(\\sin(180^\\circ - x) = \\sin x\\) to rewrite the angles:\n   \\[\n   \\sin 100^\\circ = \\sin (180^\\circ - 80^\\circ) = \\sin 80^\\circ,\n 