al answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Define the fractions\nfraction1 = 37 / 8\nfraction2 = 8 / 37\n\n# Calculate the expression\nresult = (fraction1 ** 55) * (fraction2 ** 55)\nprint(result)\n```", "To solve the expression \\(\\left(4\\dfrac{5}{8}\\right)^{55} \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{8}{37}\\right)^{55}\\), we can first convert the mixed number \\(4 \\dfrac{5}{8}\\) into an improper fraction. The mixed number \\(4 \\dfrac{5}{8}\\) is equivalent to \\(\\dfrac{37}{8}\\). Therefore, the expression becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\left(\\dfrac{37}{8}\\right)^{55} \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{8}{37}\\right)^{55}\n\\]\n\nWe can use the property of exponents \\((a^m \\cdot b^m = (a \\cdot b)^m)\\) to simplify this expression. Specifically, we can combine the fractions inside the parentheses:\n\n\\[\n\\left(\\dfrac{37}{8} \\cdot \\dfrac{8}{37}\\right)^{55}\n\\]\n\nThe product \\(\\dfrac{37}{8} \\cdot \\dfrac{8}{37}\\) simplifies to 1, so the expression becomes:\n\n\\[\n1^{55}\n\\]\n\nAny number raised to the power of 1 is 1, so the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Define the fractions\nfraction1 = 37 / 8\nfraction2 = 8 / 37\n\n# Raise the fractions to the 55th power and multiply\nresult = (fraction1 ** 55) * (fraction2 ** 55)\nprint(result)\n```", '$\\left(4\\dfrac{5}{8}\\right)^{55} \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{8}{37}\\right)^{55} = \\left(\\dfrac{37}{8}\\right)^{55} \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{8}{37}\\right)^{55} = 1^{55} = 1$', "To solve the expression \\(\\left(4\\dfrac{5}{8}\\right)^{55} \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{8}{37}\\right)^{55}\\), we can first convert the mixed number \\(4 \\dfrac{5}{8}\\) to an improper fraction. The mixed number \\(4 \\dfrac{5}{8}\\) is equivalent to \\(\\dfrac{37}{8}\\). So the expression becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\left(\\dfrac{37}{8}\\right)^{55} \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{8}{37}\\right)^{55}\n\\]\n\nWe can use the property of exponents that says \\((a/b)^n \\cdot (b/a)^n = 1\\).