\n\npossible_c_values = find_possible_c_values()\nsum_of_possible_c_values = sum(set(possible_c_values))  # Use set to avoid counting duplicates\nprint(sum_of_possible_c_values)\n```\n```output\n0\n```\nIt seems that the refined approach still did not yield any valid results. Let's take a more detailed look at the problem and the constraints.\n\nGiven the polynomial \\(P(x) = 2x^3 - 2ax^2 + (a^2 - 81)x - c\\) with roots \\(r_1, r_2, r_3\\), we have the following conditions from Vieta's formulas:\n1. \\(r_1 + r_2 + r_3 = a\\)\n2. \\(r_1r_2 + r_2r_3 + r_3r_1 = \\frac{a^2 - 81}{2}\\)\n3. \\(r_1r_2r_3 = \\frac{c}{2}\\)\n\nWe need to find the values of \\(a\\) and \\(c\\) such that there are exactly two possible values of \\(c\\) after knowing \\(a\\).\n\nLet's manually check some possible values of \\(a\\) and see if we can find the roots that satisfy the conditions.\n\nFor example, let's consider \\(a = 15\\):\n- The roots \\(r_1, r_2, r_3\\) must sum to 15.\n- The product of the roots taken two at a time must satisfy \\(r_1r_2 + r_2r_3 + r_3r_1 = \\frac{15^2 - 81}{2} = 54\\).\n- The product of the roots must satisfy \\(r_1r_2r_3 = \\frac{c}{2}\\).\n\nLet's manually check the possible combinations of roots for \\(a = 15\\):\n- If \\(r_1 = 3, r_2 = 6, r_3 = 6\\), then \\(r_1r_2 + r_2r_3 + r_3r_1 = 3 \\cdot 6 + 6 \\cdot 6 + 6 \\cdot 3 = 18 + 36 + 18 = 72 \\neq 54\\), so this combination does not work.\n- If \\(r_1 = 4, r_2 = 5, r_3 = 6\\), then \\(r_1r_2 + r_2r_3 + r_3r_1 = 4 \\cdot 5 + 5 \\cdot 6 + 6 \\cdot 4 = 20 + 30 + 24 = 74 \\neq 54\\), so this combination does not work.\n- If \\(r_1 = 5, r_2 = 5, r_3 = 5\\), then \\(r_1r_2 + r_2r_3 + r_3r_1 = 5 \\cdot 5 + 5 \\cdot 5 + 5 \\cdot 5 = 25 + 25 + 25 = 75 \\neq 54\\), so this combination does not work.\n- If \\(r_1 = 3, r_2 = 6, r_3 = 6\\), then \\(r_1r_2 + r_2r_3 + r_3r_1 = 3 \\cdot 6 + 6 \\cdot 6 + 6 \\cdot 3 = 18 + 36 + 18 = 72 \\neq 54\\), so this combination does not work.\n- If \\(r_1 = 3, r_2 = 4, r_3 = 8\\), then