 - c \\). According to Vieta\'s formulas, for a cubic polynomial \\( P(x) = 2x^3 + bx^2 + cx + d \\) with roots \\( r, s, t \\), the following relationships hold:\n\\[\nr + s + t = -\\frac{b}{2}, \\quad rs + rt + st = \\frac{c}{2}, \\quad rst = -\\frac{d}{2}\n\\]\nFor our polynomial \\( P(x) = 2x^3 - 2ax^2 + (a^2 - 81)x - c \\), we have:\n\\[\nr + s + t = a, \\quad rs + rt + st = \\frac{a^2 - 81}{2}, \\quad rst = \\frac{c}{2}\n\\]\n\nGiven that the roots \\( r, s, t \\) are positive integers, we need to find values of \\( a \\) and \\( c \\) such that these conditions are satisfied.\n\nFirst, we consider the sum of the roots:\n\\[\nr + s + t = a\n\\]\nSince \\( r, s, t \\) are positive integers, \\( a \\) must be an integer greater than or equal to 3.\n\nNext, we consider the product of the roots:\n\\[\nrst = \\frac{c}{2}\n\\]\nThus, \\( c = 2rst \\).\n\nWe also have the sum of the product of the roots taken two at a time:\n\\[\nrs + rt + st = \\frac{a^2 - 81}{2}\n\\]\n\nWe need to find values of \\( r, s, t \\) that satisfy these equations. Let\'s start by testing small values of \\( a \\).\n\n### Case \\( a = 12 \\)\n\\[\nr + s + t = 12\n\\]\nWe need to find positive integers \\( r, s, t \\) such that:\n\\[\nrs + rt + st = \\frac{12^2 - 81}{2} = \\frac{144 - 81}{2} = 31.5\n\\]\nSince \\( rs + rt + st \\) must be an integer, \\( a = 12 \\) is not a valid solution.\n\n### Case \\( a = 13 \\)\n\\[\nr + s + t = 13\n\\]\nWe need to find positive integers \\( r, s, t \\) such that:\n\\[\nrs + rt + st = \\frac{13^2 - 81}{2} = \\frac{169 - 81}{2} = 44\n\\]\nWe also need:\n\\[\nrst = \\frac{c}{2}\n\\]\n\nLet\'s test possible combinations of \\( r, s, t \\) that sum to 13 and check if their product sums to 44:\n- \\( (1, 6, 6) \\):\n  \\[\n  1 + 6 + 6 = 13, \\quad 1 \\cdot 6 + 1 \\cdot 6 + 6 \\cdot 6 = 6 + 6 + 36 = 48 \\quad (\\text{not valid})\n  \\]\n- \\( (2, 5, 6) \\):\n  \\[\n  2 + 5 + 6 = 13, \\quad 2 \\cdot 5 + 2 \\cdot 6 + 5 \\cdot 6 = 10 + 12 + 30 = 52 \\quad (\\te