h inequality separately and then find the intersection of the solutions.\n\n### Solving the first inequality: \\( 0 \\ge 54p - 144 \\)\n\n\\[\n0 \\ge 54p - 144\n\\]\n\\[\n144 \\ge 54p\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{144}{54} \\ge p\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{8}{3} \\ge p\n\\]\n\\[\np \\le \\frac{8}{3}\n\\]\n\n### Solving the second inequality: \\( 0 > 12 - 20p \\)\n\n\\[\n0 > 12 - 20p\n\\]\n\\[\n20p > 12\n\\]\n\\[\np > \\frac{12}{20}\n\\]\n\\[\np > \\frac{3}{5}\n\\]\n\n### Finding the intersection of the solutions\n\nThe first inequality gives us \\( p \\le \\frac{8}{3} \\) and the second inequality gives us \\( p > \\frac{3}{5} \\). The intersection of these two intervals is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{3}{5} < p \\le \\frac{8}{3}\n\\]\n\nSo the solution in interval notation is:\n\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{3}{5}, \\frac{8}{3} \\right]\n\\]\n\nLet's verify this using Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, solve, S\r\n\r\n# Define the variable\r\np = symbols('p')\r\n\r\n# Define the inequalities\r\ninequality1 = 0 >= 54*p - 144\r\ninequality2 = 0 > 12 - 20*p\r\n\r\n# Solve the inequalities\r\nsolution1 = solve(inequality1, p)\r\nsolution2 = solve(inequality2, p)\r\n\r\n# Find the intersection of the solutions\r\nsolution = S.Intersection(S.Interval.open(-S.Infinity, solution2[0]), S.Interval.open(solution1[0], S.Infinity))\r\nprint(solution)\n```\n```output\nAttributeError: Attribute 'Intersection' was not installed on SymPy registry S\n```\nIt seems there was an error in the code. Let's correct it by using the `sympy` library's `Interval` and `Intersection` functions properly.\n\nHere's the corrected code:\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, solve, Interval, Intersection\r\n\r\n# Define the variable\r\np = symbols('p')\r\n\r\n# Define the inequalities\r\ninequality1 = 0 >= 54*p - 144\r\ninequality2 = 0 > 12 - 20*p\r\n\r\n# Solve the inequalities\r\nsolution1 = solve(inequality1, p)\r\nsolution2 = solve(inequality2, p)\r\n\r\n# Find the interval for each solution\r\ninterv