eas of the two triangles:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{2} \\times (a + b) \\times h = 42\n   \\]\n9. Because \\(AC\\) is the altitude for both triangles, we can express \\(AC\\) in terms of \\(h\\). Since \\(AC\\) is the same for both triangles, the area of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is half of the area of the trapezoid because \\(AD\\) is one of the bases of the trapezoid:\n   \\[\n   [\\triangle ACD] = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 42 = 21\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the area of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is \\(\\boxed{21}\\).", 'Let $E$ be the intersection of $\\overline{AC}$ and $\\overline{BD}$.  Note that $\\triangle ABE \\sim \\triangle DCE$ and that $[\\triangle ABE] + [\\triangle DCE] = [\\triangle ABC] + [\\triangle ACD] = 21$.  Since $\\angle AEB$ and $\\angle DEC$ are supplementary, it follows that $\\angle ABE = \\angle DCE$ and $\\angle BAE = \\angle CDE$.  Since $\\overline{AC}$ bisects $\\angle BAD$, we have $\\angle BAE = \\angle CAD$.  Thus, $\\angle CAD = \\angle CDE$, which implies that $\\triangle ACD \\sim \\triangle DCE$.  Therefore, $[\\triangle ACD] = \\frac{[\\triangle DCE]}{[\\triangle ABE]} [\\triangle ABE] = \\frac{[\\triangle DCE]}{[\\triangle ABE]} (21 - [\\triangle DCE])$.\n\nWe can now use the fact that $[\\triangle ABE] + [\\triangle DCE] = 21$ to solve for $[\\triangle ACD]$.  Let $x = [\\triangle ACD]$.  Then we have\n\n$$[\\triangle ABE] + [\\triangle DCE] = 21$$\n\n$$[\\triangle ABE] = 21 - [\\triangle DCE]$$\n\n$$[\\triangle ACD] = \\frac{[\\triangle DCE]}{[\\triangle ABE]} (21 - [\\triangle DCE])$$\n\n$$x = \\frac{[\\triangle DCE]}{21 - [\\triangle DCE]} (21 - [\\triangle DCE])$$\n\n$$x = [\\triangle DCE]$$\n\n$$[\\triangle ACD] = [\\triangle DCE]$$\n\nSince $[\\triangle ACD] = [\\triangle DCE]$, we have $[\\triangle ACD] = \\frac{1}{2} [\\triangle ABCD] = \\frac{1}{2} (42) = \\boxed{21}$.', "Let's break down the problem step by step and use Python to ensure our calculations are accurate.\n\n1. **Understanding the Trapezoid and its Properties:**\n   - Since \\