sor (GCD) of 21 and 30 is 3, so:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{21}{30} = \\frac{21 \\div 3}{30 \\div 3} = \\frac{7}{10}\n\\]\n\nNow we can substitute this simplified fraction back into the formula:\n\n\\[\n(7)(10)\\left(\\frac{7}{10}\\right) = (7)(10)\\left(\\frac{7}{10}\\right) = (7)(10)\\left(\\frac{7}{10}\\right) = (7)(7) = 49\n\\]\n\nSo the simplified value of $\\frac{7}{30} @ \\frac{10}{21}$ is 49. Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nfrom math import gcd\n\n# Define the fractions\nm, n = 7, 30\np, q = 10, 21\n\n# Simplify q/n\ngcd_q_n = gcd(q, n)\nsimplified_q_n = q // gcd_q_n, n // gcd_q_n\n\n# Calculate the result of the operation\nresult = m * p * (simplified_q_n[0] / simplified_q_n[1])\nprint(result)\n```\n```output\n49.0\n```\nThe simplified value of $\\frac{7}{30} @ \\frac{10}{21}$ is \\(\\boxed{49}\\).", "To solve the problem, we need to apply the given operation to the fractions \\(\\frac{7}{30}\\) and \\(\\frac{10}{21}\\). The operation is defined as:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{m}{n} @ \\frac{p}{q} = (m)(p)\\left(\\frac{q}{n}\\right)\n\\]\n\nGiven the fractions \\(\\frac{7}{30}\\) and \\(\\frac{10}{21}\\), we can identify \\(m = 7\\), \\(n = 30\\), \\(p = 10\\), and \\(q = 21\\). Substituting these values into the operation, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{7}{30} @ \\frac{10}{21} = (7)(10)\\left(\\frac{21}{30}\\right)\n\\]\n\nFirst, we need to simplify \\(\\frac{21}{30}\\). The greatest common divisor (GCD) of 21 and 30 is 3, so we can simplify \\(\\frac{21}{30}\\) to \\(\\frac{7}{10}\\). Now, we substitute this simplified fraction back into the operation:\n\n\\[\n(7)(10)\\left(\\frac{7}{10}\\right) = (7)(10)\\left(\\frac{7}{10}\\right) = 7 \\cdot 7 = 49\n\\]\n\nSo the simplified value of \\(\\frac{7}{30} @ \\frac{10}{21}\\) is 49. Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nfrom math import gcd\r\n\r\n# Define the fractions\r\nm, n = 7, 30\r\np, q = 10, 21\r\n\r\n# Simplify the fraction q/n\r\ngcd_q_n = gcd(q, n)\r\nsimplified_q_n = q // gcd_q_n\r\n\r\n# Calculate the 