mplify the expression using Python. We'll define the polynomial and simplify it to check its degree.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variables\nx, a, b, c = sp.symbols('x a b c')\n\n# Define the polynomial p(x)\np_x = ((x - b)*(x - c))/((a - b)*(a - c)) + ((x - a)*(x - c))/((b - a)*(b - c)) + ((x - a)*(x - b))/((c - a)*(c - b))\n\n# Simplify the polynomial\np_x_simplified = sp.simplify(p_x)\nprint(p_x_simplified)\n```\n```output\n1\n```\nThe simplified form of the polynomial \\(p(x)\\) is \\(1\\). This confirms that \\(p(x)\\) is a constant polynomial, and therefore its degree is \\(0\\).\n\nSo, the degree of the polynomial \\(p(x)\\) is \\(\\boxed{0}\\).", "To determine the degree of the polynomial \\( p(x) \\), we need to analyze the expression:\n\n\\[ p(x) = \\frac{(x - b)(x - c)}{(a - b)(a - c)} + \\frac{(x - a)(x - c)}{(b - a)(b - c)} + \\frac{(x - a)(x - b)}{(c - a)(c - b)}. \\]\n\nLet's break this down step-by-step.\n\n1. **Identify the Degree of Each Term:**\n   Each term in the polynomial is a product of linear factors in the numerator, divided by a constant. The degree of each term in the numerator is 2 (since each term is a product of two linear factors). The denominator is a constant, so it does not affect the degree of the polynomial. Therefore, each term is a polynomial of degree 2.\n\n2. **Combine the Terms:**\n   Since each term is a polynomial of degree 2, the sum of these terms will also be a polynomial of degree 2, unless there is some cancellation that reduces the degree. However, in this case, there is no cancellation that reduces the degree because the terms are constructed in such a way that they do not cancel each other out.\n\n3. **Conclusion:**\n   The degree of the polynomial \\( p(x) \\) is 2.\n\nLet's confirm this by evaluating the polynomial at a few points and checking the degree using Python. We'll define the polynomial and check its degree.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variables\nx, a, b, c = sp.symbo