ngth of the hallway. Using the speed we calculated, we can find the length of the hallway:\n\\[ \\text{Length of the hallway} = \\text{Speed} \\times \\text{Time} = \\frac{1}{9} \\text{ meters/second} \\times 90 \\text{ seconds} = 10 \\text{ meters} \\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Constants\ntime_to_travel_1_km_hours = 2.5  # time in hours to travel 1 km\ndistance_1_km_meters = 1000  # 1 km in meters\ntime_to_travel_hallway_seconds = 90  # time in seconds to travel the hallway\n\n# Convert time to travel 1 km to seconds\ntime_to_travel_1_km_seconds = time_to_travel_1_km_hours * 3600\n\n# Calculate the speed of the robot in meters per second\nspeed_m_per_s = distance_1_km_meters / time_to_travel_1_km_seconds\n\n# Calculate the length of the hallway\nlength_of_hallway_meters = speed_m_per_s * time_to_travel_hallway_seconds\nprint(length_of_hallway_meters)\n```\n```output\n10.0\n```\nThe length of the hallway is \\(\\boxed{10}\\) meters.", "To solve for \\( b \\) given the functions \\( f(x) = x - 3 \\) and \\( q(x) = bx + 1 \\) with the condition \\( f(q(1)) = -3 \\), we can follow these steps:\n\n1. Substitute \\( x = 1 \\) into \\( q(x) \\) to find \\( q(1) \\).\n2. Substitute \\( q(1) \\) into \\( f(x) \\) to find \\( f(q(1)) \\).\n3. Set \\( f(q(1)) \\) equal to \\(-3\\) and solve for \\( b \\).\n\nLet's go through the steps one by one.\n\n1. Calculate \\( q(1) \\):\n\\[ q(1) = b \\cdot 1 + 1 = b + 1 \\]\n\n2. Substitute \\( q(1) \\) into \\( f(x) \\):\n\\[ f(q(1)) = f(b + 1) = (b + 1) - 3 = b - 2 \\]\n\n3. Set \\( f(q(1)) \\) equal to \\(-3\\) and solve for \\( b \\):\n\\[ b - 2 = -3 \\]\n\\[ b = -3 + 2 \\]\n\\[ b = -1 \\]\n\nSo, the value of \\( b \\) is \\(-1\\). Let's verify this by substituting back into the functions to ensure the result is correct.\n\nWe'll use Python to verify this solution.\n```python\n# Define the functions f and q\ndef f(x):\n    return x - 3\n\ndef q(x, b):\n    return b * x + 1\n\n# Given condition f(q(1)) = 