ts = [A, B, C, D]\n    points.sort()\n    return (points[0] == A and points[1] == B and points[2] == C and points[3] == D) or \\\n           (points[0] == A and points[1] == B and points[2] == D and points[3] == C)\n\n# Total number of ways to arrange 4 points\ntotal_arrangements = list(itertools.permutations(['A', 'B', 'C', 'D']))\n\n# Count the number of favorable arrangements\nfavorable_count = sum(1 for arrangement in total_arrangements if do_chords_intersect(*arrangement))\n\n# Total number of arrangements\ntotal_count = len(total_arrangements)\n\n# Probability calculation\nprobability = favorable_count / total_count\nprint(probability)\n```\n```output\n0.0\n```\nIt seems there was an error in the logic of the code. The function `do_chords_intersect` was not correctly checking for the intersection condition. Let's correct the function and re-run the simulation.\n\nThe correct condition for intersection is that one point of each chord should be between the other two points. So, the points should be in the order $ACBD$ or $ADBC$ when traversing the circle.\n\nHere is the corrected code:\n\n```python\nimport itertools\n\n# Function to check if chords AB and CD intersect\ndef do_chords_intersect(A, B, C, D):\n    points = [A, B, C, D]\n    points.sort()\n    return (points[0] == A and points[1] == C and points[2] == B and points[3] == D) or \\\n           (points[0] == A and points[1] == D and points[2] == B and points[3] == C) or \\\n           (points[0] == B and points[1] == C and points[2] == A and points[3] == D) or \\\n           (points[0] == B and points[1] == D and points[2] == A and points[3] == C) or \\\n           (points[0] == C and points[1] == A and points[2] == D and points[3] == B) or \\\n           (points[0] == C and points[1] == B and points[2] == D and points[3] == A) or \\\n           (points[0] == D and points[1] == A and points[2] == C and points[3] == B) or \\\n           (points[0] == D and points[1] == B and points[2] == C and points[3] =