he nth Fibonacci number modulo m\ndef fibonacci_mod(n, m):\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n    elif n == 1:\n        return 1\n    a, b = 0, 1\n    for _ in range(2, n + 1):\n        a, b = b, (a + b) % m\n    return b\n\n# Find the 100th Fibonacci number modulo 4\nremainder = fibonacci_mod(100, 4)\nprint(remainder)\n```\n```output\n3\n```\nThe remainder when the 100th term of the Fibonacci sequence is divided by 4 is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).", "To solve the problem, we need to find the 100th term of the Fibonacci sequence and then find its remainder when divided by 4. Instead of calculating the 100th term directly, which would be computationally expensive, we can look for a pattern in the remainders of the Fibonacci sequence when divided by 4.\n\nLet's generate the first few terms of the Fibonacci sequence and their remainders when divided by 4:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|}\n\\hline\nn & F_n & F_n \\mod 4 \\\\\n\\hline\n1 & 1 & 1 \\\\\n2 & 1 & 1 \\\\\n3 & 2 & 2 \\\\\n4 & 3 & 3 \\\\\n5 & 5 & 1 \\\\\n6 & 8 & 0 \\\\\n7 & 13 & 1 \\\\\n8 & 21 & 1 \\\\\n9 & 34 & 2 \\\\\n10 & 55 & 3 \\\\\n11 & 89 & 1 \\\\\n12 & 144 & 0 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nWe can see that the sequence of remainders starts to repeat every 6 terms: 1, 1, 2, 3, 1, 0. This means that the Fibonacci sequence modulo 4 is periodic with a period of 6. Therefore, to find the remainder of the 100th term when divided by 4, we need to find the position of 100 within this repeating cycle. Since the sequence repeats every 6 terms, we can find the remainder of 100 divided by 6:\n\n\\[\n100 \\div 6 = 16 \\text{ remainder } 4\n\\]\n\nThis means that the 100th term has the same remainder as the 4th term when divided by 4. From the table, we can see that the remainder of the 4th term is 3. Therefore, the remainder when the 100th term of the Fibonacci sequence is divided by 4 is 3.\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Function to find the nth Fibonacci number modulo m\r\ndef fibonacci_mod(n, m):