repeat every 4 powers. So, to find the units digit of \\(8^6\\), we can determine the position of 6 in the repeating cycle. Since the cycle repeats every 4, we find the remainder when 6 is divided by 4:\n\\[6 \\div 4 = 1 \\text{ remainder } 2\\]\nThis means that the units digit of \\(8^6\\) is the same as the units digit of \\(8^2\\), which is 4.\n\nTherefore, the units digit of \\(18^6\\) is \\(\\boxed{4}\\).", "Find the units digit of $27^5.$\n/hide] To find the units digit of \\(18^6\\), we only need to consider the units digit of the base number, which is 8 in this case. The units digit of \\(18^6\\) will be the same as the units digit of \\(8^6\\).\n\nSimilarly, to find the units digit of \\(27^5\\), we only need to consider the units digit of the base number, which is 7 in this case. The units digit of \\(27^5\\) will be the same as the units digit of \\(7^5\\).\n\nWe can determine the units digits by observing the patterns in the units digits of the powers of 8 and 7.\n\n1. For \\(8^6\\):\n   - \\(8^1 = 8\\) (units digit is 8)\n   - \\(8^2 = 64\\) (units digit is 4)\n   - \\(8^3 = 512\\) (units digit is 2)\n   - \\(8^4 = 4096\\) (units digit is 6)\n   - \\(8^5 = 32768\\) (units digit is 8)\n   - \\(8^6 = 262144\\) (units digit is 4)\n   The units digits repeat every 4 numbers: 8, 4, 2, 6. Since \\(6 \\div 4 = 1\\) remainder 2, the units digit of \\(8^6\\) is the same as the units digit of \\(8^2\\), which is 4.\n\n2. For \\(7^5\\):\n   - \\(7^1 = 7\\) (units digit is 7)\n   - \\(7^2 = 49\\) (units digit is 9)\n   - \\(7^3 = 343\\) (units digit is 3)\n   - \\(7^4 = 2401\\) (units digit is 1)\n   - \\(7^5 = 16807\\) (units digit is 7)\n   The units digits repeat every 4 numbers: 7, 9, 3, 1. Since \\(5 \\div 4 = 1\\) remainder 1, the units digit of \\(7^5\\) is the same as the units digit of \\(7^1\\), which is 7.\n\nSo, the units digit of \\(18^6\\) is 4, and the units digit of \\(27^5\\) is 7. Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Function to find the units digit of a^b\ndef units_digit(a, b):\n    # Get the units digit of the base number\n    units_digit_base = a % 10\n    \n    # Find the units digit of the power\n    if units_digit_base == 0:\n        return 0\n    elif units_digit_base == 1:\n        return 1\n    elif units_digit_base == 2:\n        pattern = [2, 4, 8, 6]\n    elif units_digit_base == 3:\n        pattern = [3, 9, 7, 1]\n    elif units_digit_base == 4:\n        pattern = [4, 6]\n    elif units_digit_base == 5:\n        return 5\n    elif units_digit_base == 6:\n        return 6\n    elif units_digit_base == 7:\n        pattern = [7, 9, 3, 1]\n    elif units_digit_base == 8:\n        pattern = [8, 4, 2, 6]\n    elif units_digit_base == 9:\n        pattern = [9, 1]\n    \n    # Determine the position in the pattern\n    if units_digit_base in [2, 3, 7, 8]:\n        position = (b - 1) % 4\n    elif units_digit_base in [4, 9]:\n        position = (b - 1) % 2\n    else:\n        position = 0\n    \n    return pattern[position]\n\n# Find the units digit of 18^6\nunits_digit_18_6 = units_digit(18, 6)\n# Find the units digit of 27^5\nunits_digit_27_5 = units_digit(27, 5)\n\nprint((units_digit_18_6, units_digit_27_5))\n```\n```output\n(4, 7)\n```\nThe units digit of \\(18^6\\) is \\(\\boxed{4}\\), and the units digit of \\(27^5\\) is \\(\\boxed{7}\\).", "The units digit of $18^6$ is the same as the units digit of $8^6$. We can find this by looking at the pattern in the units digits of the powers of 8.\n\nFirst, let's calculate the units digits of the first few powers of 8:\n- $8^1 = 8$ (units digit is 8)\n- $8^2 = 64$ (units digit is 4)\n- $8^3 = 512$ (units digit is 2)\n- $8^4 = 4096$ (units digit is 6)\n- $8^5 = 32768$ (units digit is 8)\n- $8^6 = 262144$ (units digit is 4)\n\nWe can see that the units digits repeat every 4 numbers: 8, 4, 2, 6. Since the units digit of $8^6$ is the same as the units digit of $8^{4+2}$, we can find that the units digit of $8^6$ is the same as the units digit of $8^2$, which is 4.\n\nSo, the units digit of $18^6$ is $\\boxed{4}$.", "Find the units digit of $18^7.$\nTo find the units digit of \\(18^6\\) and \\(18^7\\), we only need to consider the units digit of the base number, which is 8 in this case. The units digit of a product is determined only by the units digits of the numbers being multiplied. So, we can simplify the problem to finding the units digit of \\(8^6\\) and \\(8^7\\).\n\nLet's start by calculating the units digits of the first few powers of 8:\n\n- \\(8^1 = 8\\) (units digit is 8)\n- \\(8^2 = 64\\) (units digit is 4)\n- \\(8^3 = 512\\) (units digit is 2)\n- \\(8^4 = 4096\\) (units digit is 6)\n- \\(8^5 = 32768\\) (units digit is 8)\n- \\(8^6 = 262144\\) (units digit is 4)\n- \\(8^7 = 2097152\\) (units digit is 2)\n\nFrom the above calculations, we can see that the units digits repeat every 4 numbers: 8, 4, 2, 6. \n\nTo find the units digit of \\(8^6\\), we note that 6 is divisible by 4 (with a remainder of 2), so the units digit of \\(8^6\\) is the same as the units digit of \\(8^2\\), which is 4.\n\nTo find the units digit of \\(8^7\\), we note that 7 divided by 4 leaves a remainder of 3, so the units digit of \\(8^7\\) is the same as the units digit of \\(8^3\\), which is 2.\n\nTherefore, the units digit of \\(18^6\\) is 4, and the units digit of \\(18^7\\) is 2. Let's confirm this using Python code.\n```python\n# Function to find the units digit of 8^n\r\ndef units_digit_of_power(base, exponent):\r\n    # We only need the units digit of the base\r\n    base_units_digit = base % 10\r\n    # Calculate the units digit of base_units_digit^exponent\r\n    units_digit = (base_units_digit ** exponent) % 10\r\n    return units_digit\r\n\r\n# Find the units digit of 18^6 and 18^7\r\nunits_digit_18_6 = units_digit_of_power(18, 6)\r\nunits_digit_18_7 = units_digit_of_power(18, 7)\r\n\r\nprint((units_digit_18_6, units_digit_18_7))\n```\n```output\n(4, 2)\n```\nThe units digit of \\(18^6\\) is \\(\\boxed{4}\\), and the units digit of \\(18^7\\) is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).", 'To find the units digit of \\(18^6\\), we need to determine \\(18^6 \\mod 10\\). The units digit of a number is the remainder when the number is divided by 10. Since the units digit of 18 is 8, the units digit of \\(18^6\\) is the same as the units digit of \\(8^6\\). So, we need to find \\(8^6 \\mod 10\\).\n\nWe can simplify this by observing the pattern in the units digits of the powers of 8:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n8^1 & = 8 \\quad (\\text{units digit is } 8) \\\\\n8^2 & = 64 \\quad (\\text{units digit is } 4) \\\\\n8^3 & = 512 \\quad (\\text{units digit is } 2) \\\\\n8^4 & = 4096 \\quad (\\text{units digit is } 6) \\\\\n8^5 & = 32768 \\quad (\\text{units digit is } 8) \\\\\n8^6 & = 262144 \\quad (\\text{units digit is } 4) \\\\\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nWe can see that the units digits repeat every 4 numbers: 8, 4, 2, 6. Since the exponent 6 is 2 more than a multiple of 4 (i.e., \\(6 = 4 \\cdot 1 + 2\\)), the units digit of \\(8^6\\) is the same as the units digit of \\(8^2\\), which is 4.\n\nTherefore, the units digit of \\(18^6\\) is \\(\\boxed{4}\\).', 'Find the units digit of $24^4.$ 1. 18^6\n2. 24^4\n3. 6^6\n4. 4^4\n5. 6^6 = 46656\n6. 4^4 = 256\n7. 6 (units digit of 46656)\n8. 6 (units digit of 256)\n9. 6 + 6 = 12\n10. 2 (units digit of 12)\n11. \\boxed{2}', "Find the units digit of $19^8.$ \\boxed{} \\end{document}\n\nAssistant: To find the units digit of \\(18^6\\) and \\(19^8\\), we can simplify the problem by focusing only on the units digit of the base numbers. \n\n1. For \\(18^6\\):\n   - The units digit of 18 is 8.\n   - We need to find the units digit of \\(8^6\\).\n\n2. For \\(19^8\\):\n   - The units digit of 19 is 9.\n   - We need to find the units digit of \\(9^8\\).\n\nWe can use Python to compute these values efficiently. Let's proceed with the calculations.\n```python\n# Function to find the units digit of a number raised to a power\r\ndef units_digit(base, exponent):\r\n    # Extract the units digit of the base\r\n    units_digit_base = base % 10\r\n    # Compute the units digit of the power\r\n    units_digit_result = (units_digit_base ** exponent) % 10\r\n    return units_digit_result\r\n\r\n# Calculate the units digit of 18^6\r\nunits_digit_18_6 = units_digit(18, 6)\r\n\r\n# Calculate the units digit of 19^8\r\nunits_digit_19_8 = units_digit(19, 8)\r\n\r\nprint((units_digit_18_6, units_digit_19_8))\n```\n```output\n(4, 1)\n```\nThe units digit of \\(18^6\\) is 4, and the units digit of \\(19^8\\) is 1.\n\nTherefore, the final answers are:\n\\[\n\\boxed{4} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\boxed{1}\n\\]", "To find the units digit of \\(18^6\\), we need to determine the units digit of \\(8^6\\). This is because the units digit of a number is solely determined by the units digit of its base when raised to a power.\n\nHere are the steps to solve the problem:\n\n1. Identify the units digit of the base, which is 8 in this case.\n2. Compute the units digit of \\(8^6\\).\n\nWe can do this by calculating the units digit of the first few powers of 8 and looking for a pattern. Let's calculate the units digits of \\(8^1, 8^2, 8^3, 8^4, 8^5, 8^6\\):\n\n- \\(8^1 = 8\\) (units digit is 8)\n- \\(8^2 = 64\\) (units digit is 4)\n- \\(8^3 = 512\\) (units digit is 2)\n- \\(8^4 = 4096\\) (units digit is 6)\n- \\(8^5 = 32768\\) (units digit is 8)\n- \\(8^6 = 262144\\) (units digit is 4)\n\nWe can see that the units digits repeat every 4 powers: 8, 4, 2, 6. Since the units digit of \\(8^6\\) is the same as the units digit of \\(8^2\\), the units digit of \\(8^6\\) is 4.\n\nSo, the units digit of \\(18^6\\) is \\(\\boxed{4}\\).", "Find the units digit of $19^8.$\n/hide] To find the units digit of \\(18^6\\) and \\(19^8\\), we can focus on the units digits of the base numbers, since only the units digit of the base number affects the units digit of the power.\n\n1. The units digit of 18 is 8. So, we need to find the units digit of \\(8^6\\).\n2. The units digit of 19 is 9. So, 