 if units_digit_base in [4, 6, 5, 9, 1]:\r\n        return pattern[(b - 1) % len(pattern)]\r\n    else:\r\n        return pattern[b % len(pattern)]\r\n\r\n# Units digit of 18^6\r\nunits_digit_18_6 = units_digit(18, 6)\r\n\r\n# Units digit of 19^8\r\nunits_digit_19_8 = units_digit(19, 8)\r\n\r\nprint((units_digit_18_6, units_digit_19_8))\n```\n```output\n(4, 1)\n```\nThe units digit of \\(18^6\\) is \\(\\boxed{4}\\), and the units digit of \\(19^8\\) is \\(\\boxed{1}\\).", "To find the units digit of \\(18^6\\), we only need to consider the units digit of the base, which is 8 in this case. The units digit of \\(18^6\\) will be the same as the units digit of \\(8^6\\).\n\nWe can determine the units digit of \\(8^6\\) by observing the pattern in the units digits of the powers of 8:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n8^1 & = 8 \\quad (\\text{units digit is } 8) \\\\\n8^2 & = 64 \\quad (\\text{units digit is } 4) \\\\\n8^3 & = 512 \\quad (\\text{units digit is } 2) \\\\\n8^4 & = 4096 \\quad (\\text{units digit is } 6) \\\\\n8^5 & = 32768 \\quad (\\text{units digit is } 8) \\\\\n8^6 & = 262144 \\quad (\\text{units digit is } 4) \\\\\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nWe can see that the units digits repeat every 4 powers: 8, 4, 2, 6. Since the exponent 6 is 2 more than a multiple of 4 (i.e., \\(6 = 4 \\cdot 1 + 2\\)), the units digit of \\(8^6\\) will be the same as the units digit of \\(8^2\\), which is 4.\n\nTherefore, the units digit of \\(18^6\\) is 4. Let's confirm this using Python code.\n```python\n# Function to find the units digit of a^b\ndef units_digit(a, b):\n    # Get the units digit of the base\n    units_digit_base = a % 10\n    # Find the units digit of units_digit_base^b\n    units_digit_result = (units_digit_base ** b) % 10\n    return units_digit_result\n\n# Calculate the units digit of 18^6\nunits_digit_18_6 = units_digit(18, 6)\nprint(units_digit_18_6)\n```\n```output\n4\n```\nThe units digit of \\(18^6\\) is \\(\\boxed{4}\\).", "Find the units digit of $33^6.$  \\boxed{} 