 the right-hand side:\n\n\\[ 3 = \\frac{6}{x} \\]\n\n3. Multiply both sides by \\( x \\) to solve for \\( x \\):\n\n\\[ 3x = 6 \\]\n\n4. Divide both sides by 3:\n\n\\[ x = 2 \\]\n\nLet's verify this solution by substituting \\( x = 2 \\) back into the original equation to ensure it holds true. We'll use Python to confirm our solution.\n```python\n# Define the value of x\nx = 2\n\n# Calculate the left-hand side of the equation\nlhs = 3 + 1/x\n\n# Calculate the right-hand side of the equation\nrhs = 7/x\n\n# Check if the left-hand side equals the right-hand side\nprint((lhs, rhs, lhs == rhs))\n```\n```output\n(3.5, 3.5, True)\n```\nThe left-hand side of the equation \\( 3 + \\frac{1}{2} = 3.5 \\) is equal to the right-hand side of the equation \\( \\frac{7}{2} = 3.5 \\). Since both sides are equal, the solution \\( x = 2 \\) is correct.\n\nThus, the number is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).", "Let's denote the number by \\( x \\). The problem states that three plus the reciprocal of a number equals 7 divided by that number. This can be written as the equation:\n\n\\[ 3 + \\frac{1}{x} = \\frac{7}{x} \\]\n\nTo solve for \\( x \\), we can follow these steps:\n\n1. Subtract \\( \\frac{1}{x} \\) from both sides of the equation to isolate the term with \\( x \\) on one side.\n2. Multiply both sides by \\( x \\) to eliminate the denominators.\n3. Solve the resulting equation for \\( x \\).\n\nLet's do this step-by-step.\n\n1. Start with the equation:\n\n\\[ 3 + \\frac{1}{x} = \\frac{7}{x} \\]\n\n2. Subtract \\( \\frac{1}{x} \\) from both sides:\n\n\\[ 3 = \\frac{7}{x} - \\frac{1}{x} \\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[ 3 = \\frac{6}{x} \\]\n\n3. Multiply both sides by \\( x \\):\n\n\\[ 3x = 6 \\]\n\n4. Solve for \\( x \\):\n\n\\[ x = \\frac{6}{3} = 2 \\]\n\nSo, the number is \\( x = 2 \\). Let's verify this solution by substituting \\( x = 2 \\) back into the original equation to ensure it holds true.\n\n\\[ 3 + \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{7}{2} \\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[ 3 + 0.5 = 3.5 \\]\