2 + c1) <= 1\n\n# Case 2: c = -1\nc2 = -1\nconstraint2_c2 = sp.Abs(a + b + c2) <= 1\nconstraint3_c2 = sp.Abs(a/4 + b/2 + c2) <= 1\n\n# Case 3: a + b + c = 1\nconstraint2_1 = a + b + c - 1\nconstraint3_1 = sp.Abs(a/4 + b/2 + c) <= 1\n\n# Case 4: a + b + c = -1\nconstraint2_neg1 = a + b + c + 1\nconstraint3_neg1 = sp.Abs(a/4 + b/2 + c) <= 1\n\n# Case 5: a/4 + b/2 + c = 1\nconstraint3_2 = a/4 + b/2 + c - 1\nconstraint2_2 = sp.Abs(a + b + c) <= 1\n\n# Case 6: a/4 + b/2 + c = -1\nconstraint3_3 = a/4 + b/2 + c + 1\nconstraint2_3 = sp.Abs(a + b + c) <= 1\n\n# Solve for the maximum value of |a| + |b| + |c|\nmax_value = 0\nfor case in [c1, c2]:\n    for constraint in [constraint2_c1, constraint3_c1, constraint2_c2, constraint3_c2]:\n        solutions = sp.solve([constraint], (a, b), dict=True)\n        for sol in solutions:\n            value = expression.subs(sol).subs(c, case)\n            if value > max_value:\n                max_value = value\n\nprint(max_value)\n```\n```output\nNotImplementedError: solving Abs(a + b + 1) when the argument is not real or imaginary.\n```\nThe error occurs because SymPy's `solve` function has difficulty handling absolute value constraints directly. Instead, we can handle the absolute value constraints by considering the boundary conditions explicitly. We'll evaluate the expression at the boundary points of the constraints and check the values.\n\nLet's manually consider the boundary conditions and evaluate the expression at those points.\n\n1. **Case 1: \\( c = 1 \\)**\n   - \\( |a + b + 1| \\le 1 \\) implies \\( -1 \\le a + b + 1 \\le 1 \\), so \\( -2 \\le a + b \\le 0 \\).\n   - \\( \\left| \\frac{a}{4} + \\frac{b}{2} + 1 \\right| \\le 1 \\) implies \\( -1 \\le \\frac{a}{4} + \\frac{b}{2} + 1 \\le 1 \\), so \\( -2 \\le \\frac{a}{4} + \\frac{b}{2} \\le 0 \\).\n\n2. **Case 2: \\( c = -1 \\)**\n   - \\( |a + b - 1| \\le 1 \\) implies \\( 0 \\le a + b \\le 2 \\).\n   - \\( \\left| \\frac{a}{4} + \\frac{b}{2} - 1 \\right| \\le 1 \\) implies 