he following conditions must be satisfied:\n\n1. \\(a + b > c\\)\n2. \\(a + c > b\\)\n3. \\(b + c > a\\)\n\nIn this case, the sides of the triangle are 1, \\(x\\), and \\(y\\). Therefore, the conditions for the existence of the triangle are:\n\n1. \\(1 + x > y\\)\n2. \\(1 + y > x\\)\n3. \\(x + y > 1\\)\n\nSince \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) are chosen from the interval \\((0, 3)\\), we need to find the area in the \\(x\\)-\\(y\\) plane that satisfies these conditions. The total possible area for \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) is a square of side length 3, so the total area is \\(3 \\times 3 = 9\\).\n\nWe will use Python to calculate the area of the region in the \\(x\\)-\\(y\\) plane that satisfies the triangle inequalities. We can do this by integrating over the valid region.\n\nLet's break down the region into smaller parts and calculate the area.\n\n1. The condition \\(1 + x > y\\) implies \\(y < 1 + x\\).\n2. The condition \\(1 + y > x\\) implies \\(y > x - 1\\).\n3. The condition \\(x + y > 1\\) implies \\(y > 1 - x\\).\n\nWe need to integrate over the region where all three conditions are satisfied. We can do this by integrating from \\(x = 0\\) to \\(x = 3\\) and for each \\(x\\), integrating \\(y\\) from \\(\\max(0, x - 1, 1 - x)\\) to \\(\\min(3, 1 + x)\\).\n\nLet's write the Python code to calculate this area.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variables\nx, y = sp.symbols('x y')\n\n# Define the limits for y based on the conditions\ny_lower = sp.Max(0, x - 1, 1 - x)\ny_upper = sp.Min(3, 1 + x)\n\n# Define the integrand (which is 1 since we're finding area)\nintegrand = 1\n\n# Integrate over the region\narea = sp.integrate(sp.integrate(integrand, (y, y_lower, y_upper)), (x, 0, 3))\nprint(area.evalf())\n```\n```output\n4.50000000000000\n```\nThe area of the region in the \\(x\\)-\\(y\\) plane that satisfies the conditions for the existence of the triangle is 4.5. Since the total possible area for \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) is a square of side length 3, the total area is \\(