ive in \\((-\\infty, -2)\\).\n\n2. For \\(x = 0\\) (in the interval \\((-2, 7)\\)):\n\\[0^2 - 5(0) - 14 = -14 < 0.\\]\nSo, the expression is negative in \\((-2, 7)\\).\n\n3. For \\(x = 8\\) (in the interval \\((7, \\infty)\\)):\n\\[8^2 - 5(8) - 14 = 64 - 40 - 14 = 10 > 0.\\]\nSo, the expression is positive in \\((7, \\infty)\\).\n\nSince we are looking for the interval where \\(x^2 - 5x - 14 \\le 0\\), we include the interval where the expression is negative and the critical points themselves (since the inequality is non-strict). Therefore, the solution to the inequality is:\n\\[-2 \\le x \\le 7.\\]\nIn interval notation, this is:\n\\[\\boxed{[-2, 7]}\\]', 'To solve the inequality \\(x^2 - 5x - 4 \\le 10\\), we first rewrite it as \\(x^2 - 5x - 14 \\le 0\\). The next step is to find the roots of the equation \\(x^2 - 5x - 14 = 0\\). These roots will help us determine the intervals to test for the inequality.\n\nThe quadratic equation \\(x^2 - 5x - 14 = 0\\) can be factored as \\((x - 7)(x + 2) = 0\\). Therefore, the roots are \\(x = 7\\) and \\(x = -2\\).\n\nThese roots divide the number line into three intervals: \\((-\\infty, -2)\\), \\((-2, 7)\\), and \\((7, \\infty)\\). We need to test each interval to see where the inequality \\(x^2 - 5x - 14 \\le 0\\) holds true.\n\n1. For \\(x \\in (-\\infty, -2)\\), we can test a value like \\(x = -3\\):\n   \\[\n   (-3)^2 - 5(-3) - 14 = 9 + 15 - 14 = 10 \\not\\le 0\n   \\]\n   So, the inequality is not true for \\(x \\in (-\\infty, -2)\\).\n\n2. For \\(x \\in (-2, 7)\\), we can test a value like \\(x = 0\\):\n   \\[\n   (0)^2 - 5(0) - 14 = -14 \\le 0\n   \\]\n   So, the inequality is true for \\(x \\in (-2, 7)\\).\n\n3. For \\(x \\in (7, \\infty)\\), we can test a value like \\(x = 8\\):\n   \\[\n   (8)^2 - 5(8) - 14 = 64 - 40 - 14 = 10 \\not\\le 0\n   \\]\n   So, the inequality is not true for \\(x \\in (7, \\infty)\\).\n\nSince the inequality is true for \\(x \\in (-2, 7)\\) and the roots \\(x = -2\\) and \\(x = 7\\) make