 + 8^{38}\\) is divided by 11.\n\n3. Calculate \\(8^{34} \\mod 11\\):\nWe can use Fermat's Little Theorem, which states that \\(a^{p-1} \\equiv 1 \\mod p\\) for any integer \\(a\\) and prime \\(p\\). Here, \\(p = 11\\), so \\(8^{10} \\equiv 1 \\mod 11\\).\n\\[8^{34} = (8^{10})^3 \\cdot 8^4 \\equiv 1^3 \\cdot 8^4 \\equiv 8^4 \\mod 11\\]\n\\[8^4 = 4096 \\div 11 = 372 \\text{ remainder } 4 \\Rightarrow 8^4 \\equiv 4 \\mod 11\\]\nSo, \\(8^{34} \\equiv 4 \\mod 11\\).\n\n4. Calculate \\(8^{38} \\mod 11\\):\n\\[8^{38} = (8^{10})^3 \\cdot 8^8 \\equiv 1^3 \\cdot 8^8 \\equiv 8^8 \\mod 11\\]\n\\[8^8 = (8^4)^2 \\equiv 4^2 \\equiv 16 \\mod 11 \\equiv 5 \\mod 11\\]\nSo, \\(8^{38} \\equiv 5 \\mod 11\\).\n\n5. Finally, we add the results:\n\\[8^{34} + 8^{38} \\equiv 4 + 5 \\equiv 9 \\mod 11\\]\n\nSo, the remainder when \\(129^{34} + 96^{38}\\) is divided by 11 is \\(\\boxed{9}\\).", "To find the remainder when \\(129^{34} + 96^{38}\\) is divided by 11, we can use modular arithmetic properties. Specifically, we can reduce the bases modulo 11 first and then perform the exponentiation.\n\n1. First, we reduce 129 and 96 modulo 11:\n\\[ 129 \\mod 11 = 7 \\]\n\\[ 96 \\mod 11 = 7 \\]\n\nSo the expression \\(129^{34} + 96^{38}\\) reduces to \\(7^{34} + 7^{38}\\) modulo 11.\n\n2. Next, we can simplify \\(7^{34} \\mod 11\\) and \\(7^{38} \\mod 11\\) using Fermat's Little Theorem, which states that if \\(p\\) is a prime number and \\(a\\) is an integer not divisible by \\(p\\), then \\(a^{p-1} \\equiv 1 \\mod p\\). Since 11 is a prime number, we have:\n\\[ 7^{10} \\equiv 1 \\mod 11 \\]\n\nSo we can reduce the exponents modulo 10:\n\\[ 34 \\mod 10 = 4 \\]\n\\[ 38 \\mod 10 = 8 \\]\n\nThus, \\(7^{34} \\equiv 7^4 \\mod 11\\) and \\(7^{38} \\equiv 7^8 \\mod 11\\).\n\n3. Now we need to calculate \\(7^4 \\mod 11\\) and \\(7^8 \\mod 11\\):\n\\[ 7^2 = 49 \\equiv 5 \\mod 11 \\]\n\\[ 7^4 = (7^2)^2 \\equiv 5^2 = 25 \\equiv 3 \\mod 11 \\]\n\\[ 7^8 = (7^4)^2 \\equiv 3^2 = 9 \\mod 11 \\]\n\nSo the expression \\(7^{34} + 7^{38} \\mod 11\\) reduces to \\(3 + 9 \\mod 11\\):\n\\[ 3 + 9 = 12 \\]\n\\[ 12 \\mod 11 = 1 \\]\n\nThus, the remainder when \\(129^{34} + 96^{38}\\) is divided by 11 is \\(1\\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Function to compute (base^exponent) % mod using a fast exponentiation method\r\ndef modular_exponentiation(base, exponent, mod):\r\n    result = 1\r\n    base = base % mod\r\n    while exponent > 0:\r\n        if (exponent % 2) == 1:  # If exponent is odd, multiply base with result\r\n            result = (result * base) % mod\r\n        exponent = exponent >> 1  # Divide the exponent by 2\r\n        base = (base * base) % mod  # Square the base\r\n    return result\r\n\r\n# Given values\r\nbase1 = 129\r\nbase2 = 96\r\nexponent1 = 34\r\nexponent2 = 38\r\nmod = 11\r\n\r\n# Reduce bases modulo 11\r\nbase1_mod = base1 % mod\r\nbase2_mod = base2 % mod\r\n\r\n# Compute the modular exponentiations\r\nresult1 = modular_exponentiation(base1_mod, exponent1, mod)\r\nresult2 = modular_exponentiation(base2_mod, exponent2, mod)\r\n\r\n# Sum the results and take modulo 11\r\nfinal_result = (result1 + result2) % mod\r\nprint(final_result)\n```", "To find the remainder when \\(129^{34} + 96^{38}\\) is divided by 11, we can use modular arithmetic. Specifically, we can simplify the problem by finding the remainders of 129 and 96 when divided by 11, and then using those remainders to compute the powers.\n\nFirst, we find the remainders:\n\\[ 129 \\div 11 = 11 \\text{ remainder } 8 \\Rightarrow 129 \\equiv 8 \\pmod{11} \\]\n\\[ 96 \\div 11 = 8 \\text{ remainder } 8 \\Rightarrow 96 \\equiv 8 \\pmod{11} \\]\n\nSo, we can rewrite the expression \\(129^{34} + 96^{38}\\) as:\n\\[ 129^{34} + 96^{38} \\equiv 8^{34} + 8^{38} \\pmod{11} \\]\n\nNext, we need to find the values of \\(8^{34} \\mod 11\\) and \\(8^{38} \\mod 11\\). To simplify this, we can use Fermat's Little Theorem, which states that if \\(p\\) is a prime number and \\(a\\) is an integer not divisible by \\(p\\), then \\(a^{p-1} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{p}\\). Here, \\(p = 11\\), so \\(8^{10} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{11}\\).\n\nUsing this, we can simplify \\(8^{34}\\) and \\(8^{38}\\):\n\\[ 8^{34} = (8^{10})^3 \\cdot 8^4 \\equiv 1^3 \\cdot 8^4 \\equiv 8^4 \\pmod{11} \\]\n\\[ 8^{38} = (8^{10})^3 \\cdot 8^8 \\equiv 1^3 \\cdot 8^8 \\equiv 8^8 \\pmod{11} \\]\n\nNow we need to find \\(8^4 \\mod 11\\) and \\(8^8 \\mod 11\\):\n\\[ 8^2 = 64 \\equiv 9 \\pmod{11} \\]\n\\[ 8^4 = (8^2)^2 \\equiv 9^2 = 81 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{11} \\]\n\\[ 8^8 = (8^4)^2 \\equiv 4^2 = 16 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{11} \\]\n\nSo, we have:\n\\[ 8^{34} + 8^{38} \\equiv 8^4 + 8^8 \\equiv 4 + 5 \\equiv 9 \\pmod{11} \\]\n\nThus, the remainder when \\(129^{34} + 96^{38}\\) is divided by 11 is \\(9\\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{9}\n\\]\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Function to compute (base^exponent) % mod using a fast exponentiation method\r\ndef modular_exponentiation(base, exponent, mod):\r\n    result = 1\r\n    base = base % mod\r\n    while exponent > 0:\r\n        if (exponent % 2) == 1:  # If exponent is odd, multiply base with result\r\n            result = (result * base) % mod\r\n        exponent = exponent >> 1  # Divide exponent by 2\r\n        base = (base * base) % mod  # Square the base\r\n    return result\r\n\r\n# Given values\r\nbase1 = 129\r\nbase2 = 96\r\nexponent1 = 34\r\nexponent2 = 38\r\nmod = 11\r\n\r\n# Simplify the bases modulo 11\r\nbase1_mod = base1 % mod\r\nbase2_mod = base2 % mod\r\n\r\n# Compute the modular exponentiations\r\nresult1 = modular_exponentiation(base1_mod, exponent1, mod)\r\nresult2 = modular_exponentiation(base2_mod, exponent2, mod)\r\n\r\n# Sum the results and take modulo 11\r\nfinal_result = (result1 + result2) % mod\r\nprint(final_result)\n```", "To find the remainder when \\(129^{34} + 96^{38}\\) is divided by 11, we can use modular arithmetic. Specifically, we can reduce the bases modulo 11 first and then perform the exponentiation and addition.\n\nFirst, we reduce the bases modulo 11:\n\\[ 129 \\equiv 129 - 11 \\cdot 11 \\equiv 129 - 121 \\equiv 8 \\pmod{11} \\]\n\\[ 96 \\equiv 96 - 11 \\cdot 8 \\equiv 96 - 88 \\equiv 8 \\pmod{11} \\]\n\nSo the expression \\(129^{34} + 96^{38}\\) is equivalent to \\(8^{34} + 8^{38} \\pmod{11}\\).\n\nNext, we need to find the values of \\(8^{34} \\pmod{11}\\) and \\(8^{38} \\pmod{11}\\). We can use Fermat's Little Theorem, which states that if \\(p\\) is a prime number and \\(a\\) is an integer not divisible by \\(p\\), then \\(a^{p-1} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{p}\\). Here, \\(p = 11\\) and \\(a = 8\\), so \\(8^{10} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{11}\\).\n\nUsing this, we can simplify the exponents:\n\\[ 8^{34} = (8^{10})^3 \\cdot 8^4 \\equiv 1^3 \\cdot 8^4 \\equiv 8^4 \\pmod{11} \\]\n\\[ 8^{38} = (8^{10})^3 \\cdot 8^8 \\equiv 1^3 \\cdot 8^8 \\equiv 8^8 \\pmod{11} \\]\n\nNow we need to find \\(8^4 \\pmod{11}\\) and \\(8^8 \\pmod{11}\\):\n\\[ 8^2 = 64 \\equiv 64 - 5 \\cdot 11 \\equiv 64 - 55 \\equiv 9 \\pmod{11} \\]\n\\[ 8^4 = (8^2)^2 \\equiv 9^2 \\equiv 81 \\equiv 81 - 7 \\cdot 11 \\equiv 81 - 77 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{11} \\]\n\\[ 8^8 = (8^4)^2 \\equiv 4^2 \\equiv 16 \\equiv 16 - 11 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{11} \\]\n\nSo we have:\n\\[ 8^{34} + 8^{38} \\equiv 4 + 5 \\equiv 9 \\pmod{11} \\]\n\nTherefore, the remainder when \\(129^{34} + 96^{38}\\) is divided by 11 is \\(\\boxed{9}\\).", "To find the remainder when \\(129^{34} + 96^{38}\\) is divided by 11, we can use modular arithmetic. First, we need to simplify the bases 129 and 96 modulo 11.\n\n1. Calculate \\(129 \\mod 11\\):\n\\[129 \\div 11 = 11 \\text{ remainder } 8 \\Rightarrow 129 \\equiv 8 \\mod 11\\]\n\n2. Calculate \\(96 \\mod 11\\):\n\\[96 \\div 11 = 8 \\text{ remainder } 8 \\Rightarrow 96 \\equiv 8 \\mod 11\\]\n\nSo, the expression \\(129^{34} + 96^{38}\\) simplifies to \\(8^{34} + 8^{38} \\mod 11\\).\n\nNext, we need to simplify \\(8^{34} \\mod 11\\) and \\(8^{38} \\mod 11\\). We can use Fermat's Little Theorem, which states that if \\(p\\) is a prime number and \\(a\\) is an integer not divisible by \\(p\\), then \\(a^{p-1} \\equiv 1 \\mod p\\). Here, \\(p = 11\\) and \\(a = 8\\), so \\(8^{10} \\equiv 1 \\mod 11\\).\n\n3. Simplify \\(8^{34} \\mod 11\\):\n\\[8^{34} = (8^{10})^3 \\cdot 8^4 \\equiv 1^3 \\cdot 8^4 \\equiv 8^4 \\mod 11\\]\n\n4. Simplify \\(8^{38} \\mod 11\\):\n\\[8^{38} = (8^{10})^3 \\cdot 8^8 \\equiv 1^3 \\cdot 8^8 \\equiv 8^8 \\mod 11\\]\n\nNow we need to calculate \\(8^4 \\mod 11\\) and \\(8^8 \\mod 11\\):\n\\[8^2 = 64 \\equiv 9 \\mod 11\\]\n\\[8^4 = (8^2)^2 \\equiv 9^2 = 81 \\equiv 4 \\mod 11\\]\n\\[8^8 = (8^4)^2 \\equiv 4^2 = 16 \\equiv 5 \\mod 11\\]\n\nSo, \\(8^{34} + 8^{38} \\equiv 4 + 5 \\equiv 9 \\mod 11\\).\n\nThe remainder when \\(129^{34} + 96^{38}\\) is divided by 11 is \\(\\boxed{9}\\).", "To solve the problem of finding the remainder when \\(129^{34} + 96^{38}\\) is divided by 11, we can use modular arithmetic. Specifically, we can reduce the bases 129 and 96 modulo 11 first, and then compute the powers modulo 11.\n\n1. Compute \\(129 \\mod 11\\):\n\\[129 \\div 11 = 11 \\text{ remainder } 8 \\Rightarrow 129 \\equiv 8 \\mod 11\\]\n\n2. Compute \\(96 \\mod 11\\):\n\\[96 \\div 11 = 8 \\text{ remainder } 8 \\Rightarrow 96 \\equiv 8 \\mod 11\\]\n\nSo, the expression \\(129^{34} + 96^{38} \\mod 11\\) simplifies to \\(8^{34} + 8^{38} \\mod 11\\).\n\n3. Next, we need to compute \\(8^{34} \\mod 11\\) and \\(8^{38} \\mod 11\\). We can use Fermat's Little Theorem, which states that if \\(p\\) is a prime number and \\(a\\) is an integer not divisible by \\(p\\), then \\(a^{p-1} \\equiv 1 \\mod p\\). Here, \\(p = 11\\) and \\(a = 8\\), so \\(8^{10} \\equiv 1 \\mod 11\\).\n\n4. Using Fermat's Little Theorem:\n\\[8^{34} = (8^{10})^3 \\cdot 8^4 \\equiv 1^3 \\cdot 8^4 \\equiv 8^4 \\mod 11\\]\n\\[8^{38} = (8^{10})^3 \\cdot 8^8 \\equiv 1^3 \\cdot 8^8 \\equiv 8^8 \\mod 11\\]\n\n5. Now we need to compute \\(8^4 \\mod 11\\) 