ind the matrix $\\mathbf{M}$ first. Let's denote the matrix $\\mathbf{M}$ as:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{M} = \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\nGiven that:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{M} \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 9 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathbf{M} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 7 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\nThis gives us the following system of equations:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n2a - b = 9 \\\\\n2c - d = 3 \\\\\na - 3b = 7 \\\\\nc - 3d = -1\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nWe can solve this system of equations to find the values of $a, b, c,$ and $d$. Let's solve it step by step.\n\nFirst, solve the first pair of equations for $a$ and $b$:\n\\[\n2a - b = 9 \\quad \\text{(1)}\n\\]\n\\[\na - 3b = 7 \\quad \\text{(2)}\n\\]\nMultiply equation (2) by 2 and subtract it from equation (1):\n\\[\n2a - b - 2(a - 3b) = 9 - 2 \\cdot 7 \\implies 2a - b - 2a + 6b = 9 - 14 \\implies 5b = -5 \\implies b = -1\n\\]\nSubstitute $b = -1$ into equation (1):\n\\[\n2a - (-1) = 9 \\implies 2a + 1 = 9 \\implies 2a = 8 \\implies a = 4\n\\]\nNext, solve the second pair of equations for $c$ and $d$:\n\\[\n2c - d = 3 \\quad \\text{(3)}\n\\]\n\\[\nc - 3d = -1 \\quad \\text{(4)}\n\\]\nMultiply equation (4) by 2 and subtract it from equation (3):\n\\[\n2c - d - 2(c - 3d) = 3 - 2(-1) \\implies 2c - d - 2c + 6d = 3 + 2 \\implies 5d = 5 \\implies d = 1\n\\]\nSubstitute $d = 1$ into equation (3):\n\\[\n2c - 1 = 3 \\implies 2c = 4 \\implies c = 2\n\\]\nSo the matrix $\\mathbf{M}$ is:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{M} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\nNow, we need to find the image of the line $y = 2x + 1$ under $\\mathbf{M}$. Any point on the line $y = 2x + 1$ can be written as $\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ 2x + 1 \\end{pmatrix}$. We need to find the image of this point under $\\mathbf{M}$:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{M} \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ 2x + 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ 2x + 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4x - (2x + 1) \\\\ 2x + (2x + 1) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2x - 1 \\\\ 4x + 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\nSo the image of the point $(x, 2x + 1)$ under $\\mathbf{M}$ is $(2x - 1, 4x + 1)$. We need to express this in the form $y = mx + b$. Let's denote the image point as $(x', y')$:\n\\[\nx' = 2x - 1 \\implies x = \\frac{x' + 1}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\ny' = 4x + 1 = 4\\left(\\frac{x' + 1}{2}\\right) + 1 = 2(x' + 1) + 1 = 2x' + 2 + 1 = 2x' + 3\n\\]\nSo the equation of the image of the line $y = 2x + 1$ under $\\mathbf{M}$ is:\n\\[\ny = 2x + 3\n\\]\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the matrix M\na, b, c, d = 4, -1, 2, 1\nM = sp.Matrix([[a, b], [c, d]])\n\n# Define the line equation y = 2x + 1\nx = sp.symbols('x')\ny = 2*x + 1\n\n# Define the point on the line\npoint = sp.Matrix([x, y])\n\n# Compute the image of the point under M\nimage_point = M * point\n\n# Extract the x' and y' coordinates\nx_prime = image_point[0]\ny_prime = image_point[1]\n\n# Solve for x in terms of x'\nx_in_terms_of_x_prime = sp.solve(x_prime - sp.symbols('x_prime'), x)[0]\n\n# Substitute x in terms of x' into the y_prime equation\ny_in_terms_of_x_prime = y_prime.subs(x, x_in_terms_of_x_prime)\n\n# Simplify the equation\ny_in_terms_of_x_prime_simplified = sp.simplify(y_in_terms_of_x_prime)\nprint(y_in_terms_of_x_prime_simplified)\n```\n```output\n2*x_prime + 3\n```\nThe Python code confirms that the image of the line $y = 2x + 1$ under the matrix $\\mathbf{M}$ is the line $y = 2x + 3$. Therefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{y = 2x + 3}\n\\]", "To determine the number of ways to select 5 students from a group of 6 students, we can use the concept of combinations. The number of ways to choose \\(k\\) items from \\(n\\) items without regard to the order of selection is given by the binomial coefficient, which is calculated as:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{n}{k} = \\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}\n\\]\n\nIn this problem, \\(n = 6\\) and \\(k = 5\\). So 