inus_1 += (-1)**(n-i) * roots[i]\n    return 2 * a_n_minus_1\n\n# Possible factorizations of 33\nfactorizations = [\n    [33, 1],\n    [11, 3],\n    [-33, -1],\n    [-11, -3]\n]\n\n# Calculate |a_{n-1}| for each factorization\nresults = [(abs(calculate_a_n_minus_1(roots)), roots) for roots in factorizations]\n\n# Find the minimum |a_{n-1}|\nmin_abs_a_n_minus_1 = min(results, key=lambda x: x[0])\n\nprint(min_abs_a_n_minus_1)\n```\n```output\n(16, [11, 3])\n```\nThe least possible value of \\(|a_{n-1}|\\) is \\(\\boxed{16}\\). This occurs when the roots of the polynomial are 11 and 3.", "To solve this problem, we need to use the properties of polynomials with integer coefficients and integer roots. Given that the polynomial $f(x) = 2x^n + a_{n-1}x^{n-1} + \\cdots + a_2x^2 + a_1x + 66$ has distinct integer roots, we can use Vieta's formulas, which relate the coefficients of the polynomial to sums and products of its roots.\n\nLet the roots of the polynomial be $r_1, r_2, \\ldots, r_n$. By Vieta's formulas, the sum of the roots taken one at a time (with the leading coefficient factored out) is given by:\n\\[ r_1 + r_2 + \\cdots + r_n = -\\frac{a_{n-1}}{a_n} = -\\frac{a_{n-1}}{2}. \\]\nThe product of the roots is given by:\n\\[ r_1 r_2 \\cdots r_n = (-1)^n \\frac{a_0}{a_n} = (-1)^n \\frac{66}{2} = (-1)^n \\cdot 33. \\]\n\nSince the roots are distinct integers, the product of the roots must be $\\pm 33$. The possible sets of distinct integer roots whose product is $\\pm 33$ are:\n\\[ \\{1, -1, 3, -3, 11, -11, 33, -33\\}. \\]\n\nWe need to find the combination of roots that minimizes $|a_{n-1}|$. To do this, we need to find the combination of roots that gives the smallest absolute value for the sum of the roots.\n\nLet's consider the possible combinations of roots and calculate the corresponding $|a_{n-1}|$.\n\n1. If the roots are $\\{1, -1, 3, -3, 11, -11, 33, -33\\}$, the sum of the roots is $0$, so $|a_{n-1}| = 0$.\n2. If the roots are $\\{1, -1, 3, -3, 11, -11\\}$, the